2 CONT Fortún Garcés of Pamplona
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT 	This article does not cite any references or sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please help improve this article by adding citations to reliable sources. Unsourced material may be challenged and removed. (December 2009)
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortún Garcés (Arabic: فرتون بن قرسية‎, Fortoûn ibn Garsiya, d. after 925), called the One-Eyed or the Monk, was the king of Pamplona from 882 to 905.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of King García Íñiguez, himself the son of king Íñigo Íñiguez Arista, the founder of the kingdom, and Fortún was to be the last king of the Arista dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Fortún was taken prisoner by the Moors in 860 during the invasion of Emir Mohammed I of Córdoba and kept for the next 20 years. While a prisoner in Córdoba, his daughter Oneca Fortúnez married Abdallah ibn Mohammed, who wou
2 CONC ld later succeed his father as Emir. He was released from captivity in 880 and returned to Pamplona, apparently accompanied by his daughter. He succeeded upon the death of his father at Ayhar in 882 in a battle against Emir Mohamm
2 CONC ed I.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known of the remainder of his reign except the manner of its ending. In 905, an alliance of the Banu Qasi under Lubb ibn Mohammed, King Alfonso III of Asturias, and count Raymond I of Pallars brought about a successful c
2 CONC oup in favor of the latter's nephew, Sancho Garcés, son of García Jiménez of "another part of the kingdom", forcing Fortún to retire as a monk to Leyra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortún had several surviving children by his wife Oria, whose parentage has been subject to much speculation:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Íñigo Fortúnez
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Aznar Fortúnez
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Blasco (Velasco) Fortúnez
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Lope Fortúnez
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Oneca Fortúnez, who married firstly Abdallah ibn Mohammed, Emir of Córdoba, and secondly her cousin Aznar Sánchez of Larraun, grandson of king García Íñiguez, becoming the mother of the future queens Toda Aznárez, wife of Sa
2 CONC ncho Garcés, and Sancha Aznárez, wife of king Jimeno Garcés, as well as grandmother of caliph Abd-ar-Rahman III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT García Íñiguez 	King of Pamplona
2 CONT
2 CONT 882–905 	Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho Garcés I
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 3 March 2010 at 18:29.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Fortún Garcés
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT
2 CONT Fortún Garcés, rey de Pamplona (870-905). Último rey de la dinastía Iñiga.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hijo del rey García Iñiguez y de la reina consorte Urraca, posiblemente hija de Fortún Ibn Musa. Fue conocido como «el Monje» o «el Tuerto».
2 CONT
2 CONT Estuvo preso en Córdoba desde que fue capturado en el 860, tras la invasión de Pamplona por parte de Mohamed I, hasta poco antes de la muerte de su padre, en calidad de rehén, al no estar capacitado para las artes marciales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Durante su reinado sufrió varias expediciones de castigo por parte de los ejércitos de Córdoba y de sus aliados del Ebro, los Banu Qasi, que ya habían superado las controversias anteriores con la capital cordobesa y actuaban nueva
2 CONC mente como verdaderos conversos del Islam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pero posteriormente entabló buena relación con el Banu Qasi Lope ibn Muhammad, por lo que el rey Alfonso III de Asturias y el conde de Pallars, enemigos de los Banú Qasi, organizaron un "golpe de estado" por el que ocupó el trono 
2 CONC Sancho Garcés I, hijo de García Jiménez, quien había sido regente durante el cautiverio.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se cree que Fortún Garcés acabó sus días en el Monasterio de Leyre en 906.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casado posiblemente en 845 con Oria (Aurea), de filiación desconocida.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Hijos 
2 CONT
2 CONT Iñigo Fortúnez. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Aznar Fortúñez de Pamplona. 
2 CONT
