1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 'Abd al-Rahmân II al-Mutawassit ibn al-Hakam, amir al-Qurtubi
2 CONT
2 CONT b. 792, d. 22 September 852
2 CONT
2 CONT Father	al-Hakam I al-Rabdi ibn Hishâm, amir al-Qurtubi1,2 b. 771, d. 822
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother	Halawah (?)1 b. circa 776
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Abd al-Rahmân II al-Mutawassit ibn al-Hakam, amir al-Qurtubi was the (great?) grandson of his namesake, founder of the Umayyad dynasty in Spain.3 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was named in full: `Abdul-Rahman bin Al-Hakam bin Hisham.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the fourth Umayyad ruler of Muslim Spain who enjoyed a reign of brilliance and prosperity, the importance of which has been underestimated by some historians.4 Of the Umayyad. He was born in 792.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of al-Hakam I al-Rabdi ibn Hishâm, amir al-Qurtubi and Halawah (?).1,2 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Abd al-Rahmân II al-Mutawassit ibn al-Hakam, amir al-Qurtubi inherited a pacified but disgruntled kingdom from his father in 822.5 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a witness where Alfonso II Fruelez "el Casto", rey de Asturias suffered attacks by 'Abd al-Rahmân II of Córdoba between 822 and 842.5 
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Abd al-Rahmân II al-Mutawassit ibn al-Hakam, amir al-Qurtubi was able to mount attacks against Alfonso II of Asturias, and against the Franks as well, between 822 and 842.5 
2 CONT
2 CONT Emir of Córdoba at Al-Andalus, Spain, between 822 and 852.2,6,5,4 
2 CONT
2 CONT He suppressed all revolutions that took place during his reign and paid great attention to construction, his castle was characterized by its royal grandeur and the Abbasid ornamentation.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT He built several mosques and castles, and was a patron of the poets.2 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was forced to deal with a major Berber and Neo-Muslim revolt in the Mérida region in 828.5 He died on 22 September 852 at age 60 years.7
2 CONT
2 CONT * Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT # Muhammad I ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, amir al-Qurtubi+ b. c 826, d. 8861
2 CONT
