2 CONC f Toulouse, married Count Pierre of Melgueil She was still Pons' wife in April 1053, but shortly thereafter Almodis was abducted by Ramon Berenguer I, Count of Barcelona. He kidnapped her from Narbonne with the aid of a fleet sent
2 CONC  north by his ally, the Muslim emir of Tortosa. They married immediately (despite the fact both of her previous husbands were still alive) and they appear with their twin sons in a charter the next year. Pope Victor II excommunica
2 CONC ted Almodis and Ramon for this illegal marriage until 1056. Together they produced four children: Berenguer Ramon II, Count of Barcelona Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Barcelona Inés of Barcelona, married Count Guigues I of Albon Sa
2 CONC ncha of Barcelona, married Count Guillermo Ramon I of Cerdagne Almodis maintained contact with her former husbands and many children, and in 1066/1067 she traveled to Toulouse for her daughter's wedding. A few years before, in 106
2 CONC 0, Hugh V of Lusignan had revolted against his lord, Duke William VIII of Aquitaine, in support of Almodis' son William IV of Toulouse. Her sons supported one another in military campaigns; Hugh VI of Lusignan, Raymond IV of Toulo
2 CONC use, and Berenguer Ramon all took the Cross. Her third husband Ramon had a son from a previous marriage, Pedro Ramon, who was his heir. Pedro apparently resented Almodis' influence and was concerned she was trying to replace him w
2 CONC ith her own two sons. He murdered her in October 1071. Pedro was disinherited and exiled for his crime, and fled the country. When his father died in 1076, Barcelona was split between Berenguer Ramon and Ramon Berenguer, Almodis' 
2 CONC sons. The family history of murder did not end with Pedro Ramon, as Berenguer Ramon earned his nickname "The Fratricide" when he killed his own twin brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Chronicles of the abbey of St. Maixent (pub. 1886 by A. Richard) Reilly, B.F. The Conquest of Christian and Muslim Spain, 1992
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Almodis de la Marche (990 or c. 1020 – 16 October 1071) was the daughter of Bernard I, Count of Marche and wife Amélie. She married Hugh V of Lusignan around 1038 and they had two sons and one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Hugh VI of Lusignan (c. 1039-1101)
2 CONT     * Jordan de Lusignan
2 CONT     * Mélisende de Lusignan (b. bef. 1055), married before 1074 to Simon I "l'Archevêque", Vidame de Parthenay
2 CONT
2 CONT Almodis and Hugh of Lusignan divorced due to consanguinity, and Hugh arranged for her to marry Count Pons of Toulouse in 1040. Together they produced several children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * William IV of Toulouse
2 CONT     * Raymond IV of Toulouse
2 CONT     * Hugh, Abbot of Saint-Gilles
2 CONT     * Almodis of Toulouse, married Count Pierre of Melgueil
2 CONT
2 CONT She was still Pons' wife in April 1053, but shortly thereafter Almodis was abducted by Ramon Berenguer I, Count of Barcelona. He kidnapped her from Narbonne with the aid of a fleet sent north by his ally, the Muslim emir of Tortos
2 CONC a. They married immediately (despite the fact both of her previous husbands were still alive) and they appear with their twin sons in a charter the next year. Pope Victor II excommunicated Almodis and Ramon for this illegal marria
2 CONC ge until 1056. Together they produced four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Berenguer Ramon II, Count of Barcelona
2 CONT     * Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Barcelona
2 CONT     * Inés of Barcelona, married Count Guigues I of Albon
2 CONT     * Sancha of Barcelona, married Count Guillermo Ramon I of Cerdagne
2 CONT
2 CONT Almodis maintained contact with her former husbands and many children, and in 1066/1067 she traveled to Toulouse for her daughter's wedding. A few years before, in 1060, Hugh V of Lusignan had revolted against his lord, Duke Willi
2 CONC am VIII of Aquitaine, in support of Almodis' son William IV of Toulouse. Her sons supported one another in military campaigns; Hugh VI of Lusignan, Raymond IV of Toulouse, and Berenguer Ramon all took the Cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her third husband Ramon had a son from a previous marriage, Pedro Ramon, who was his heir. Pedro apparently resented Almodis' influence and was concerned she was trying to replace him with her own two sons. He murdered her in Octo
2 CONC ber 1071. Pedro was disinherited and exiled for his crime, and fled the country. When his father died in 1076, Barcelona was split between Berenguer Ramon and Ramon Berenguer, Almodis' sons. The family history of murder did not en
2 CONC d with Pedro Ramon, as Berenguer Ramon earned his nickname "The Fratricide" when he killed his own twin brother
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ***NOTE: SOME records claim Death date is c. 1111-1112.  However, other records show death in 1083 as death date in her young 20s which would be soon after marriage to Count Raymond Berenger II. At least most records and profile m
2 CONC anagers concur with DOB c. 1055-1059***
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Barcelona From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguers's marriages and descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT First wife, Aimeris of Narbonne Second wife, Mahalta (or Maud) of Apulia, born ca. 1059, died 1111/1112, daughter of Duke Robert Guiscard and of Sikelgaita de Salerno Ramon Berenguer III the Great, count of Barcelona and Provence 
2 CONC (before 1082-1131)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Almodis de la Marche (990 or c. 1020 – 16 October 1071) was the daughter of Bernard I, Count of Marche and wife Amélie. She married Hugh V of Lusignan around 1038 and they had two sons and one daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh VI of Lusignan (c. 1039-1101) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jordan de Lusignan 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mélisende de Lusignan (b. bef. 1055), married before 1074 to Simon I "l'Archevêque", Vidame de Parthenay 
2 CONT
2 CONT Almodis and Hugh of Lusignan divorced due to consanguinity, and Hugh arranged for her to marry Count Pons of Toulouse in 1040. Together they produced several children, including:
2 CONT
2 CONT William IV of Toulouse 
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond IV of Toulouse 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh, Abbot of Saint-Gilles 
2 CONT
2 CONT Almodis of Toulouse, married Count Pierre of Melgueil 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was still Pons' wife in April 1053, but shortly thereafter Almodis was abducted by Ramon Berenguer I, Count of Barcelona. He kidnapped her from Narbonne with the aid of a fleet sent north by his ally, the Muslim emir of Tortos
2 CONC a. They married immediately (despite the fact both of her previous husbands were still alive) and they appear with their twin sons in a charter the next year. Pope Victor II excommunicated Almodis and Ramon for this illegal marria
2 CONC ge until 1056. Together they produced four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguer Ramon II, Count of Barcelona 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Barcelona 
2 CONT
2 CONT Inés of Barcelona, married Count Guigues I of Albon 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha of Barcelona, married Count Guillermo Ramon I of Cerdagne 
2 CONT
2 CONT Almodis maintained contact with her former husbands and many children, and in 1066/1067 she traveled to Toulouse for her daughter's wedding. A few years before, in 1060, Hugh V of Lusignan had revolted against his lord, Duke Willi
2 CONC am VIII of Aquitaine, in support of Almodis' son William IV of Toulouse. Her sons supported one another in military campaigns; Hugh VI of Lusignan, Raymond IV of Toulouse, and Berenguer Ramon all took the Cross.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her third husband Ramon had a son from a previous marriage, Pedro Ramon, who was his heir. Pedro apparently resented Almodis' influence and was concerned she was trying to replace him with her own two sons. He murdered her in Octo
2 CONC ber 1071. Pedro was disinherited and exiled for his crime, and fled the country. When his father died in 1076, Barcelona was split between Berenguer Ramon and Ramon Berenguer, Almodis' sons. The family history of murder did not en
2 CONC d with Pedro Ramon, as Berenguer Ramon earned his nickname "The Fratricide" when he killed his own twin brother.
