1 NOTE KING OF CASTILE 1065-1072
1 NOTE He reigned from 1065 until 1072 when he was assassinated.
1 NOTE Sancho II (1036/1038-October 7, 1072), called the Strong, or in Spanish, el Fuerte, was King of Castile (1065-1072) and León (1072).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of Ferdinand I of Castile and Sancha of León. His maternal grandparents were Alfonso V of Leon by Elvira Mendes. He was married to Alberta, a woman, probably foreign, of unknown origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded in Castile while his younger brother Alfonso succeeded in their mother's inheritance of León and Galicia was given to the youngest son García.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1068, Sancho defeated his cousins Sancho IV of Navarre and Sancho of Aragón in the War of the Three Sanchos. He reconquered Bureba, Alta Rioja, and Álava which his father had given to Sancho of Navarre's father, García, in r
2 CONC eturn for aid against Bermudo III of León. In that year, he defeated Alfonso, his brother, at Llantada, but he soon teamed up with him to conquer Galicia. They succeeded (1071) and partitioned it, but Sancho then turned on Alfons
2 CONC o. With the aid of his alférez El Cid, he defeated Alfonso at Golpejera (1072). He then forced him into exile in Toledo and took over León as king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some Leonese resistance still persisted, and his sister, Urraca, Lady of Zamora, held that city against his rule. He had surrounded the city and begun a siege, when a Zamoran noble, named Vellido Dolfos, assasinated Sancho on 6 Oc
2 CONC tober 1072. Vellido had gained entry to Sancho's camp pretending to be a deserter, and sought a private conference with Sancho to tell him the weakness of the Zamoran defence. Once before Sancho, however, he used the king's own sw
2 CONC ord to impale him in the back. Fleeing, he was chased back to Zamora by El Cid but escaped into the town through a gateway since called Portillo del Traidor ("gateway of the traitor"). Sancho was succeeded in his kingdoms by the b
2 CONC rother he had previously deposed, Alfonso.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Reilly, Bernard F. 1988. The Kingdom of León-Castilla under King Alfonso VI, 1065-1109. Princeton: Princeton University Press.
