1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guigues_I_of_Albon
2 CONT
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00048720&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT He is listed as the husband of Adelaide Beaujeu with no known date or location of marriage.  Then, listed as the husband of Ines Barcelona married May 10, 1070.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/burgkvien.htm#GuiguesIdied1075B<br/>
2 CONT GUIGUES [III] "Vetus/Vieux", son of GUIGUES [II] & his wife [Gotelene ---] ([995/1000]-Cluny 22 Apr [1074/75]).  "Rodolfus rex" jointly with "Agiltrude regina coniuge nostra nec non archiepiscopo Burchardo fratre nostro et comitib
2 CONC us Rodulpho et Uberto" gave the castle of Moras to "Umberto episcopo eiusque matri domine Freburgie et nepotibus eius, Wigonis bone memorie filiis, Umberto Wigoni Willelmo" by charter dated 6 Jun 1009[177].  His birth date range, 
2 CONC estimated consistent with the birth date ranges which are estimated for the other members of this family, means that Guigues was probably still a child at the date of this charter.  "…Wigonis et Umberti filiorum fratris eius…" sig
2 CONC ned the charter dated 1012 under which "Umbertus Grationopolensis ecclesiæ præsul" donated property to the abbey of Saint-Chaffre[178].  Comte d’Albon.  "Wigo comes filius Gotelenne" donated "ecclesiam de Visilia" to Cluny by unda
2 CONC ted charter, subscribed by "Wigonis filii sui, Ainardi de Domena, duobus de fratribus suis Attanulfi Wigonis, Bermundi de Aurel, Humberti nepotis sui"[179].  This charter is dated to [1090] in the compilation of Cluny charters.  T
2 CONC he document refers to Hugues abbot of Cluny, who is named in documents between 1048 and 1090.  If [1090] is the correct date, it would have been signed towards the end of the lives of all three of the Domène brothers who subscribe
2 CONC d the document: they are named in charters for Domène between [1027] and [1085][180].  It is therefore possible that the document dates from earlier in the abbacy of Hugues, in which case the donor could be Guigues [III] "Vetus". 
2 CONC  If this was correct, it would also resolve the problem of the identity of Gotelene.  Chorier’s Histoire de Dauphiné suggests that "Gotelenne" was a corruption derived from the word "Catalonia" and therefore that this charter refe
2 CONC rs to Guigues [III]’s son by Ines de Barcelona[181].  This seems an unlikely explanation as the name "Catalonia" has not been observed in contemporary charters to describe Barcelona and adjacent counties collectively (see the docu
2 CONC ment CATALONIA).  A charter dated 29 Apr 1050 records the donation by "Guigoni…maiori, qui postea effectus est monachus…et Adelai comitissæ, et alii Guigoni, filio illius Guigonis…et uxori eius Petronillæ" to Saint-Pierre de Vienn
2 CONC e[182].  "…Guigo comes et filius eius Guigo…" witnessed the charter dated 27 Jan 1052 which records an agreement between the archbishop of Vienne and the canons of the church of Romans[183].  "Vuigo comes" donated property "in par
2 CONC rochia Heroneii" to Domène by undated charter which names "dominus Hugo Prior de Domina" (whose priorship is dated to [1058] to [1072], indicating that the donor was Guigues [III] "Vetus")[184].  "Guigo comes qui nomine vocor sene
2 CONC x atque filius meus Guigo pinguis" donated property to Oulx by charter dated 1063[185].  “Wigo” (signed “Wigonis comitis”) granted “sponse mee…Agnetis” various properties “in dotalitio”, including “castellum Albionem…Moratum…et Va
2 CONC llem…in comitatu Viennensis” by charter dated “VI Id Mai, luna XXma VIa regnante Henrico rege”, subscribed by “Pontii episcopi”[186], the date corresponding to the year 1070.  He became a monk at Cluny.  The necrology of the Prior
2 CONC y of Saint-Martin-des-Champs records the death "X Kal Mai" of "Wigo comes"[187].  The necrology of the priory of Saint-Robert records the death "X Kal Mai" of "Guigo comes qui cognominatus est Vetus"[188].  No document has yet bee
2 CONC n found which identifies the year of his death.  However, considering the date of his second marriage, and the birth of his son by his second wife, it is likely that he died in [1074/75].  He was dead by 12 Nov 1076, the date of t
2 CONC he testament of his father-in-law Ramon Berenguer Comte de Barcelona which provides for the reversion of the testator’s county to "filium Guigonis de Albion quem habuit de filia sua Agnes" in case of extinction of his descendants 
2 CONC in the male line[189].  This wording implies that both Ines and Guigues were deceased at the time.  Prudhomme distinguishes two individuals in place of "Guigues [III]"[190].  He states that the older Guigues died in 1034, married 
2 CONC "Gotelenne", and that his son was the second person, Guigues "Vetus".  He cites no primary source in support of his theory, in particular no document which indicates that one of the counts Guigues died in 1034.  The division of th
2 CONC is person into two seems unnecessary to explain the primary sources.  The family relationships shown in this document leave no primary source reference unexplained.  In any case, it seems appropriate that Guigues "Vetus" should be
2 CONC  identified with Guigues [III] as he would have well merited his nickname, assuming that his birth date is correctly estimated as shown above. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly ADELSINDIS, daughter of --- (-after 29 Apr 1050).  "Malleni episcopi Gratianopolitani et Wigonis comitis et uxoris eius Adelsendis, filiorumque suorum Umberti et Wigonis" signed a charter dated 26 Aug 1035 relating
2 CONC  to the donation of four churches in Vico to the abbey of Saint-Chaffre[191].  According to Europäische Stammtafeln[192], the first wife of Guigues [III] was "Gotelana de Clérieux".  It appears that this hypothesis must be based o
2 CONC n the charter dated [1090] under which "Wigo comes filius Gotelenne" donated property to Cluny[193].  However, as noted above, a better interpretation of the documentation is that "Gotelenne" was the mother of Guigues [III] not hi
2 CONC s wife.  A charter dated 29 Apr 1050 records the donation by "Guigoni…maiori, qui postea effectus est monachus…et Adelai comitissæ, et alii Guigoni, filio illius Guigonis…et uxori eius Petronillæ" to Saint-Pierre de Vienne[194]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (10 May 1070) INES de Barcelona, daughter of RAMON BERENGUER I "el Viejo" Comte de Barcelona & his third wife Almodis de la Marche ([1055/56]-[before 12 Nov 1076]).  “Wigo” (signed “Wigonis comitis”) granted “spon
2 CONC se mee…Agnetis” various properties “in dotalitio”, including “castellum Albionem…Moratum…et Vallem…in comitatu Viennensis” by charter dated “VI Id Mai, luna XXma VIa regnante Henrico rege”, subscribed by “Pontii episcopi”[195], th
2 CONC e date corresponding to the year 1070.  Her parentage is confirmed by the publication of the testament of "comitis Barchinone…Raimundi Berengarii" dated 12 Nov 1076 includes a residuary provision that, in case of the death of his 
2 CONC three other children, his counties would revert to "filium Guigonis de Albion quem habuit de filia sua Agnes"[196].  The wording of the testament implies that both Ines and her husband were deceased at the time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Guigues [III] & his first wife had two children:
2 CONT *1.         HUMBERT ([1025/30]-[1080]). ...Bishop of Grenoble .  
2 CONT *2.         GUIGUES [IV] "Pinguis" ([1025/30]-19 Jan [1106], bur Priory of Saint-Robert). ...Comte d'Albon. -        see below.  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Guigues [III] & his second wife had one child:
2 CONT *3.         GUIGUES dit Raymond ([1074/75]-5 Dec after 1096).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Guigues [III] had two [probably illegitimate] children by [an unknown mistress]:
2 CONT *4.          RICHARD (-after 1079).
2 CONT *5.          ARMAND (-after 1079).  
