1 NOTE {geni:about_me} García Ramírez de Navarra
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT
2 CONT García Ramírez (n. ? - † Lorca (Navarra), 21 de noviembre de 1150), rey de Navarra (1134-1150). Primer rey que abandona definitivamente el título de Rey de Pamplona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Estatua de García Ramírez en Pamplona (1750-53).
2 CONT
2 CONT Elegido por los magnates y obispos navarros como rey al no acatar las disposiciones testamentarias de Alfonso I, "el Batallador".
2 CONT
2 CONT Hijo del infante Ramiro Sánchez, señor de Monzón, y de Cristina, hija del Cid Campeador.
2 CONT
2 CONT Parece que buscando una solución a la separación de los reinos de Aragón y Navarra, que habían sido gobernados conjuntamente desde la muerte de Sancho, "el de Peñalén", hasta la muerte de Alfonso, "el Batallador", propusieron que 
2 CONC el rey-monje de Aragón fuese el "padre" y García Ramírez el "hijo". Los dos conservarían su respectivo reino, sin embargo, la primacía sobre el pueblo sería de Ramiro.
2 CONT
2 CONT Al entrar en Zaragoza Alfonso VII de León y Castilla y rendirle vasallaje los aragoneses, se acentúa la separación entre los dos reinos, que en su día fueron feudo de Sancho Garcés III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aliado con Alfonso I de Portugal, en 1137 se enfrentó a Alfonso VII de Castilla, a quien había prestado vasallaje. Firmaron la paz entre 1139 y 1140.
2 CONT
2 CONT Los navarros ocuparon Tauste en el 1146, pero Alfonso VII actuó como árbitro entre los dos reinos.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1144, después de enviudar, se casó con una hija de Alfonso, Urraca, siendo a partir de esa fecha parientes del emperador leonés-castellano los reyes de Navarra y Aragón; Ramón Berenguer era cuñado y García Ramírez yerno. Ambos 
2 CONC se reconocieron vasallos del emperador Alfonso VII.
2 CONT
2 CONT A partir de esa fecha vemos a García Ramírez auxiliando a Alfonso VII en sus campañas de reconquista, concretamente en la campaña de Almería, que fue conquistada por las tropas cristianas en 1147.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1149 firmó un tratado de paz con Ramón Berenguer, por el cual el catalán se casaría con su hija Blanca, a pesar de estar prometido con Petronila de Aragón, pero al morir García Ramírez no se llevó a cabo el compromiso.
2 CONT
2 CONT Murió el 21 de noviembre de 1150 en Lorca, cerca de Estella.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casado después de 1130 con Margarita de l'Aigle, tuvieron como descendencia a: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI, "El Sabio", rey de Navarra, casado con Sancha de Castilla. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanca de Navarra, n. después de 1133, que se casó con Sancho III de Castilla "El Deseado". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margarita de Navarra, casada con Guillermo I, Rey de Sicilia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Casado en segundas nupcias el 24 de junio de 1144, en León, con Urraca, hija bastarda del rey Alfonso VII de León y Castilla y de Guntroda, teniendo como descendencia a: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancha de Navarra, casada con Gastón V, Vizconde de Béarn, fallecido en 1170. Casada en segundas nupcias con el conde Pedro Manrique de Lara, II Señor de Molina y Mesa, XIII Vizconde de Narbona 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodrigo Garcés, Conde de Montescaglioso. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Casado en terceras nupcias con Ganfreda López. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecesor:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso I de Aragón Rey de Navarra
2 CONT
2 CONT 1134 - 1150 Sucesor:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho VI 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT García was born in the early twelfth century, the grandson of Rodrigo Díaz, better known as El Cid. His father was Ramiro Sánchez of Monzón, a son of Sancho Garcés, illegitimate son of García Sánchez III of Navarre and half-brothe
2 CONC r of Sancho IV. His mother was Cristina Rodríguez Díaz de Vivar, the Cid's daughter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime after 1130, but before his succession, García married Marguerite de l'Aigle. She was to bear him a son and successor, Sancho VI, as well as two daughters who each married kings: the elder, Blanca, born after 1133, married
2 CONC  Sancho III of Castile, while the younger, Margaret, named after her mother, married William I of Sicily. García's relationship with his first queen was, however, shaky. She took on many lovers and showed favouritism to her French
2 CONC  relatives. She bore a second son named Rodrigo, whom her husband refused to recognise as his own.[8] On 24 June 1144, in León, García married Urraca, called "La Asturiana" (the Asturian), illegitimate daughter of Alfonso VII by G
2 CONC untroda Pérez, to strengthen his relationship with his overlord.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1136, García was obliged to surrender Rioja to Castile but, in 1137, he allied with Alfonso I of Portugal and confronted Alfonso VII. They confirmed a peace between 1139 and 1140. He was thereafter an ally of Castile in the Rec
2 CONC onquista and was instrumental in the conquest of Almería in 1147. In 1146, he occupied Tauste, which belonged to Aragon, and Alfonso VII intervened to mediate a peace between the two kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to Urraca, García had also become a brother-in-law of Raymond Berengar IV, with whom he confirmed a peace treaty in 1149. The count was promised to García's daughter Blanca while already engaged to Petronilla of Ar
2 CONC agon, but García died before the marriage could be carried out.
2 CONT
2 CONT García died on 21 November 1150 in Lorca, near Estella, and was buried in the cathedral of Santa María in Pamplona. He was succeeded by his eldest son. He left one daughter by Urraca: Sancha, who married Gaston V of Béarn. He left
2 CONC  a widow in the person of his third wife, Ganfreda López.
2 CONT
2 CONT García left, as the primary monument of his reign, the monastery of Sant María de La Oliva in Carcastillo. It is a fine example of Romanesque architecture.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT García Ramírez of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garc%C3%ADa_Ram%C3%ADrez_of_Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT García Ramírez, sometimes García IV,V, VI or VII (died 21 November 1150, Lorca), called the Restorer (Spanish: el Restaurador), was Lord of Monzón and Logroño, and, from 1134, King of Navarre. He "restored" the independence of the
2 CONC  Navarrese crown after 58 years of union with the Kingdom of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT García was born in the early twelfth century, the grandson of Rodrigo Díaz, better known as El Cid. His father was Ramiro Sánchez of Monzón, a son of Sancho Garcés, illegitimate son of García Sánchez III of Navarre and half-brothe
2 CONC r of Sancho IV. His mother was Cristina Rodríguez Díaz de Vivar, the Cid's daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Rise to power
2 CONT
2 CONT When Aragon, which had from 1076 been united to Navarre, lost its warrior king Alfonso the Battler and fell into a succession crisis in 1134, García managed to wrest Navarre from his Aragonese cousins. He was elected in Pamplona b
2 CONC y the bishops and nobles of the realm against the will of Alfonso. That Alfonso, in drawing up a will, had ignored his distant relation (of an illegitimate line), is not unsurprising given the circumstances. Alfonso had nearer mal
2 CONC e kin in the form of his brother Ramiro. Besides that, since Alfonso seems to have disregarded Ramiro as well, the choice of an illegitimate descendant of Sancho the Great would undoubtedly have aroused the opposition of the Papac
2 CONC y to the succession.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramiro did succeed Alfonso in Aragon, because the nobles refused to enact the late king's unusual will. His accession did raise protest from Rome and was not uncontested within Aragon, much less in Navarre, where García was the ch
2 CONC osen candidate once the testament of Alfonso was laid aside. Rome does not seem to have opposed him, but neither does he seem to have had much support within Aragon, while Ramiro strongly objected to his election in Navarre. In li
2 CONC ght of this, the Bishop of Pamplona granted García his church's treasure to fund his government against Ramiro's pretensions.[2] Among Garcías other early supporters were Lop Ennechones, Martinus de Leit, and Count Latro, who carr
2 CONC ied out negotiations on the king's behalf with Ramiro.[3] Eventually, however, the two monarchs reached a mutual accord — the Pact of Vadoluongo — of "adoption" in January 1135: García was deemed the "son" and Ramiro the "father" 
2 CONC in an attempt to maintain both the independence of each kingdom and the de facto supremacy of the Aragonese one.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1135, García declared himself a vassal of Alfonso VII. This simultaneously put him under the protection and lordship of Castile and bought recognition of his royal status from Alfonso, who was a claimant to the Battler's su
2 CONC ccession.[4] García's submission to Castile has been seen as an act of protection for Navarre which had the consequence of putting her in an offensive alliance against Aragon, which thus forced Ramiro to marry, to forge an allianc
2 CONC e with Raymond Berengar IV of Barcelona and to produce an heir, now that García, his adoptive son, was out of the question.[5] On the other hand, García may have been responding to Ramiro's marriage, which proved beyond a doubt th
2 CONC at the king of Aragon was seeking another heir than his distant relative and adopted son.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Before September 1135, Alfonso VII granted García Zaragoza as a fief.[7] Recently conquered from Aragon, this outpost of Castilian authority in the east was clearly beyond the military capacity of Alfonso to control and provided f
2 CONC urther reasons for recognition of García in Navarre in return for not only his homage, but his holding Zaragoza on behalf of Castile. In 1136, Alfons was forced to do homage for Zaragoza to Ramiro and to recognise him as King of Z
2 CONC aragoza. In 1137, Zaragoza was surrendered to Raymond Berengar, though Alfonso retained suzerainty over it. By then, García's reign in Zaragoza had closed.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]García's heirs
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime after 1130, but before his succession, García married Marguerite de l'Aigle. She was to bear him a son and successor, Sancho VI, as well as two daughters who each married kings: the elder, Blanca, born after 1133, married
2 CONC  Sancho III of Castile, while the younger, Margaret, named after her mother, married William I of Sicily. García's relationship with his first queen was, however, shaky. She took on many lovers and showed favouritism to her French
2 CONC  relatives. She bore a second son named Rodrigo, whom her husband refused to recognise as his own.[8] On 24 June 1144, in León, García married Urraca, illegitimate daughter of Alfonso VII and Guntroda Pérez, to strengthen his rela
2 CONC tionship with his overlord.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1136, García was obliged to surrender Rioja to Castile but, in 1137, he allied with Alfonso I of Portugal and confronted Alfonso VII. They confirmed a peace between 1139 and 1140. He was thereafter an ally of Castile in the Rec
2 CONC onquista and was instrumental in the conquest of Almería in 1147. In 1146, he occupied Tauste, which belonged to Aragon, and Alfonso VII intervened to mediate a peace between the two kingdoms.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to Urraca, García had also become a brother-in-law of Raymond Berengar IV, with whom he confirmed a peace treaty in 1149. The count was promised to García's daughter Blanca while already engaged to Petronilla of Ar
2 CONC agon, but García died before the marriage could be carried out.
2 CONT
2 CONT García died on 21 November 1150 in Lorca, near Estella, and was buried in the cathedral of Santa María in Pamplona. He was succeeded by his eldest son. He left one daughter by Urraca: Sancha, who married Gaston V of Béarn. He left
2 CONC  a widow in the person of his third wife, Ganfreda López.
2 CONT
2 CONT García left, as the primary monument of his reign, the monastery of Sant María de La Oliva in Carcastillo. It is a fine example of Romanesque architecture.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Lourie, Elena. "The Will of Alfonso I, 'El Batallador,' King of Aragon and Navarre: A Reassessment." Speculum, Vol. 50, No. 4. (Oct., 1975), pp 635–651.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grassotti, H. "Homenaje de García Ramírez a Alfonso VII." Principe de Viana. 94–95 (1964).
2 CONT
2 CONT Norwich, John Julius. The Kingdom in the Sun 1130-1194. Longmans: London, 1970.
2 CONT
2 CONT [
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT García VI Ramírez (Garsias Ranimiriz, also García IV, because he was only the fourth García of the Jiménez dynasty; died 21 November 1150, Lorca), called the Restorer (Spanish: el Restaurador), was Lord of Monzón and Logroño, and,
2 CONC  from 1134, King of Navarre. He "restored" the independence of the Navarrese crown after 58 years of union with the Kingdom of Aragon.
2 CONT
