2 CONT Sancho III de Castilla
2 CONT
2 CONT De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sancho_III_de_Castilla 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho III de Castilla (* Toledo, 1134 - † Toledo, 31 de agosto de 1158). Llamado "el Deseado" fue rey de Castilla desde el 21 de agosto de 1157 hasta su muerte.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hijo primogénito de Alfonso VII y Berenguela de Barcelona, el 21 de agosto de 1157 a la muerte de su padre, heredó el Reino de Castilla mientras que su hermano, Fernando II, recibió el reino de León. La división del reino entre lo
2 CONC s dos hijos de Alfonso VII no derivó en conflicto entre los hermanos debido a la intervención de su hermanastra Sancha que intermedió para que ambos respetaran los límites territoriales de cada reino.
2 CONT
2 CONT El 30 de enero de 1151 contrajo matrimonio en Laguardia con Blanca Garcés de Navarra, hija del rey navarro García Ramírez I y de Margarita de Aguila. De este matrimonio nacería, en 1155, el futuro Alfonso VIII en cuyo parto fallec
2 CONC ería Blanca.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1158 contribuyó a la creación de la Orden de Calatrava cuando los templarios rehusaron mantener la defensa de la ciudad fronteriza de Calatrava que les había sido concedida por Alfonso VI en 1147. Sancho entregó entonces la ten
2 CONC encia y el señorío de Calatrava al abad Raimundo de Fitero y al caballero Diego Velázquez, que fundarían la citada Orden militar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Siguiendo la política de su padre logró que, en 1157, el rey navarro Sancho VI, y en 1158, el conde de Barcelona Ramón Berenguer IV le prestaran vasallaje. Ese mismo año entró en el reino leonés de su hermano Fernando II pero no l
2 CONC legó a enfrentarse a él, ya que ambos monarcas el 23 de mayo de 1158 llegaron a un acuerdo firmado el tratado de Sahagún, por el cual Sancho se comprometía a devolver las tierras fronterizas entre ambas reinos que había conquistad
2 CONC o a cambio de que estas fueran dadas en señoríos a nobles leoneses cercanos al rey castellano. También acordaron prestarse ayuda mutua frente a terceros, se repartieron las zonas de conquista sobre los territorios musulmanes y est
2 CONC ablecieron que si alguno de ellos fallecía sin descendencia el reino del finado pasaría al superviviente.
2 CONT
2 CONT La súbita muerte de Sancho dejó el tratado firmado con su hermano sin fuerza alguna. Fue sucedido en el trono por su hijo Alfonso, entonces menor de edad, lo que originó una lucha por el poder en Castilla entre la familia Lara y l
2 CONC a familia Castro.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se halla enterrado en la Capilla Mayor de la catedral de Santa María de Toledo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendencia con Blanca Garcés de Navarra:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Alfonso VIII
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho III of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sancho_III_of_Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho III of Castile (1134 – 31 August 1158) was King of Castile and Toledo for one year, from 1157 to 1158. During the Reconquista, in which he took an active part, he founded the Order of Calatrava. He was called el Deseado (th
2 CONC e Desired) due to his position as the first child of his parents, born after eight years of childless marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of King Alfonso VII of Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona. During his father's reign, he appears as "king of Nájera" as early as 1149. His father's will partitioned the kingdom between his two sons: Sancho i
2 CONC nherited the kingdoms of Castile and Toledo, and Fernando inherited Leon. The two brothers had just signed a treaty when Sancho suddenly died in the summer of 1158, being buried at Toledo. He had married in 1151 to Blanca of Navar
2 CONC re, daughter of García Ramírez of Navarre, having two sons, his successor Alfonso VIII of Castile, and infante García, who died at birth in 1156, apparently also resulting in the death of Blanca. There may also have been an older 
2 CONC son who died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho III of Castile (1134 – August 31, 1158), called el Deseado (the Desired) due to his birth, as first child of his parents, not until eight years after their marriage, was King of Castile and Toledo for one year, from 1157 to
2 CONC  1158.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of King Alfonso VII of Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona. During his father's reign, he appears as "king of Najera", as early as 1149. His father's will partitioned the kingdom between his two sons: Sancho 
2 CONC inherited the kingdoms of Castile and Toledo, and Fernando inherited Leon. The two brothers had just signed a treaty when Sancho suddenly died in the summer of 1158, being buried at Toledo. He had married in 1151 to Blanca of Nava
2 CONC rre, daughter of García Ramírez of Navarre, having two sons, his successor Alfonso VIII of Castile, and infante García, who died at birth in 1156, apparently also resulting in the death of Blanca. There may also have been an older
2 CONC  son who died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT fue rey de Castilla desde el 21 de agosto de 1157 hasta su muerte.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho III of Castile (1134 – 31 August 1158) was King of Castile and Toledo for one year, from 1157 to 1158. During the Reconquista, in which he took an active part, he founded the Order of Calatrava.[1] He was called el Deseado 
2 CONC (the Desired) due to his position as the first child of his parents, born after eight years of childless marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of King Alfonso VII of León and Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona.[2] During his father's reign, he appears as "king of Nájera" as early as 1149. His father's will partitioned the kingdom between his two so
2 CONC ns: Sancho inherited the kingdoms of Castile and Toledo, and Fernando inherited Leon. The two brothers had just signed a treaty when Sancho suddenly died in the summer of 1158, being buried at Toledo.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married in 1151 to Blanca of Navarre, daughter of García Ramírez of Navarre, having two sons;
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso VIII of Castile, his successor
2 CONT
2 CONT infante García, who died at birth in 1156, apparently also resulting in the death of Blanca.
2 CONT
2 CONT There may also have been an older son who died in infancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Conant, Kenneth John, Carolingian and Romanesque architecture, 800 to 1200, (Yale University Press, 1959), 311.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.^ Busk, M. M., The history of Spain and Portugal from B.C. 1000 to A.D. 1814, (Baldwin and Cradock, 1833), 31.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.^ O'Callaghan, Joseph F., A History of Medieval Spain, (Cornell University Press, 1975), 235.
2 CONT
