2 CONT Sancho and Ferdinand II of León, from a Privilegium Imperatoris of Alfonso VII of León and Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sancho III of Castile (1134 – 31 August 1158) was King of Castile and Toledo for one year, from 1157 to 1158. During the Reconquista, in which he took an active part, he founded the Order of Calatrava.[1] He was called el Deseado 
2 CONC (the Desired) due to his position as the first child of his parents, born after eight years of childless marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the eldest son of King Alfonso VII of León and Castile and Berenguela of Barcelona.[2] During his father's reign, he appears as "king of Nájera" as early as 1149. His father's will partitioned the kingdom between his two so
2 CONC ns: Sancho inherited the kingdoms of Castile and Toledo, and Fernando inherited Leon. The two brothers had just signed a treaty when Sancho suddenly died in the summer of 1158, being buried at Toledo.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had married in 1151 to Blanca of Navarre, daughter of García Ramírez of Navarre, having two sons;
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Alfonso VIII of Castile, his successor
2 CONT
2 CONT     * infante García, who died at birth in 1156, apparently also resulting in the death of Blanca.
2 CONT
2 CONT There may also have been an older son who died in infancy.
2 CONT
