1 OCCU księcia śląskiego i zwierzchniego księcia Polski (Princesa Silesiana y Princesa Soberana de Polonia); Reina Consorte de Castilla, León y Galicia (1152-117) ; Condesa Consorte de Provenza (1161-1166) ; Condesa Consorte de Tolosa (1
2 CONC 166-1183)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://lostfort.blogspot.no/2010/07/kugelsburg-part-1-rise-of-counts-of.html
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LBHB-3X9
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richeza_von_Everstein
2 CONT
2 CONT Richeza of Poland (c. 1140 – June 16, 1185), was the daughter of King Wladislaus II the Exile of Poland, and Agnes of Babenberg. She accompanied her father into exile in 1146. Her name is also spelled Ryksa, Rixa, and Richilda.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married King Alfonso VII of Castile and León in 1152. Their only surviving child, Infanta Sancha of Castile, was born 1155. In Spain she was known as "Queen Ricca".
2 CONT
2 CONT When Alphonso died in 1157, Richeza remarried to Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Provence, in 1162. Their daughter was Douce (died 1172). Ramon Berengar died in 1166, and she remarried once more to Count Raymond V of Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her daughter, Sancha of Castile, married King Alfonso II of Aragon in 1174. Sancha's daughter, Eleanor married Count Raymond VI of Toulouse in 1202.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riquilda_de_Polonia
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Richeza of Poland also known as of Silesia (Polish: Ryksa Polska or Śląska; b. ca. 1140 - d. 16 June 1185), was a Polish princess member of the House of Piast in the Silesian branch and by her marriages Queen of León and Castile, 
2 CONC Countess of Provence and Countess of Everstein.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the third child and only daughter of King Władysław II the Exile, High Duke of Poland and ruler of Silesia, by his wife Agnes of Babenberg, daughter of Margrave Leopold III of Austria and half-sister of King Conrad III of 
2 CONC Germany.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Life 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.1 Queen of Castile and León
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.2 Countess of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.3 Countess of Everstein
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Life
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Queen of Castile and León
2 CONT
2 CONT Born and raised for the first years of her life in Poland, Richeza accompanied her parents and brothers into exile in 1146. They established themselves firstly in Bohemia and later in Germany, under the care of King Conrad III, wh
2 CONC o give his deposed brother-in-law the Saxon district of Altenburg as his residence.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1151 came the news that the King Alfonso VII of León and Castile wanted to make an alliance with the Kingdom of Germany through a wedding. Richenza, niece of King Conrad III, was the most appropriate candidate to be the wife of
2 CONC  the Iberian ruler. Between October and December 1152 Richeza and King Alfonso VII were married. In Castile she was known as Queen Riquilda (Spanish: Reina Riquilda). Her first child, Infante Ferdinand of Castile, was born in the 
2 CONC city of Toledo one year later, in 1153. Two years later, in 1155, Richeza gave birth to her second child, Infanta Sancha of Castile. King Alfonso VII died suddenly in the middle of the war against the Moors in Sierra Morena on 21 
2 CONC August 1157. Apparently, Infante Ferdinand died soon before his father.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Countess of Provence
2 CONT
2 CONT The late King divided his domains between his two surviving sons born from his first marriage to Berenguela of Barcelona: Sancho III obtained Castile and Ferdinand II received León. The relationship between Richeza and her stepson
2 CONC s wasn't good, especially after King Sancho III declared war on Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona, father of Alfonso (later King of Aragon), who was betrothed to Richeza's daughter Sancha. The unstable relations of King Ferdi
2 CONC nand II with the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick Barbarossa (cousin of Richeza) and the Antipope Victor IV added further difficulties to the Dowager Queen, who finally decided to move to the Kingdom of Aragon in 1159.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Aragonese court, Richeza met Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Provence, nephew of Count of Barcelona. Although they soon fell in love, their union would be clearly political. Ramon Berenguer II supported Victor IV against Pope 
2 CONC Alexander III, who, in turn, supported King Louis VII of France. The county of Provence was in a strategic location, between France and the Italian Peninsula. Frederick Barbarossa also wanted to win to his side to Count Ramon Bere
2 CONC nguer IV, who entered in an alliance with the Kings of France, Castile and León. In contrast, Ramon Berenguer II, soon cousin by marriage of the Emperor gained prestige and could face the pretentions of Count Hugh of Baux, who had
2 CONC  just received the Imperial Provence as a fief.
2 CONT
2 CONT Premarital negotiations lasted almost a year and a half. Between January and October 1161 Richeza and Count Ramon Berenguer II were finally married. They had only one daughter, Douce of Provence, born ca. 1162. Ramon Berenguer II 
2 CONC was killed during the siege of Nice in 1166.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after her second husband's death, plans for a new marriage for Richeza began. Apparently, she was betrothed to Raymond V, Count of Toulouse by her cousin the Emperor Frederick Barbarossa around 1166; at the same time, the now
2 CONC  Countess Douce II of Provence was engaged to the future Raymond VI. Count Raymond V wanted with this engagement to become more closely to the Hohenstaufen dynasty and took full control over the County of Provence. However, the fi
2 CONC rm opposition of King Alfonso II of Aragon (future Richeza's son-in-law) soon cancelled both betrothals, and with the help of the Genoese, began the war against Raymond V, who lasted eight years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources stated that in fact Richeza and Raymond V were married; however, this event is refuted by the majority of modern historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Countess of Everstein
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1167, Richeza married her third and last husband, Count Albert III of Everstein (b. ca. 1135 - d. ca. 1202) (who fought at the side of Frederick Barbarossa in his wars against the Guelphs) and moved to Germany with her new husb
2 CONC and. After that, she was known as Countess Richeza of Everstein (German: Gräfin Richeza von Everstein). From this union were certainly born two sons, Counts Albert IV and Konrad II of Everstein, although in some sources the existe
2 CONC nce of other three sons called Otto, Louis and Hermann of Everstein was stated.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about the later life of Richeza. She died in 1185.
2 CONT
