1 OCCU Señora de Caylar & Ansouis, Condesa de Forcalquier (1209-1213) & (1217-1220), Condesa de Provenza (1193-1213), Regente de Provenza (1213-1220), Countess of Provence
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ====FMG: Provence [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/provaixmar.htm#GersendeSabranMAlfonsoIIProvence Aix, Marseille]
2 CONT * 3. '''RAINON''' [I] '''de Sabran''' (-after 30 Nov 1209). Seigneur du Caylar et d'Ansouis. “Garsendis uxor quondam Ildefonsi comitis Provinciæ” donated her rights “in comitatu Forcalqueriensi”, granted by “Guillelmo quondam comi
2 CONC te Forcalqueriensi avo meo”, to “Raymundo Berengario filio meo” with “filiæ meæ sororis tuæ Garsendis” as substitute should he die, with the consent of “patre meo Raines de Castelar”, by charter dated 30 Nov 1209[358].  
2 CONT * '''m''' firstly ( [1178] ) '''GARSINDE de Forcalquier''', daughter of GUILLAUME [II] Comte de Forcalquier & his wife Adelais de Béziers (-before 1193). Her parentage is confirmed by the charter dated [3] Jun 1202 under which "Wi
2 CONC llelmo…comite Forcalquerii filio dominæ comitissæ veterannæ" confirmed the marriage of "Delfino filio dominæ Beatricis…ducissæ Burgundiæ, Albonensis ac Viennensis comitissæ" and "neptem suam…filiam filiæ suæ…Beatricem"[359]. The p
2 CONC rimary source which confirms her name has not been identified. '''m''' secondly ---. The name of Rainon’s second wife is not known. 
2 CONT * '''Rainon [I] & his first wife had two children:''' 
2 CONT ** a) '''GERSENDE de Sabran'''.  '''Ctss de Forcalquier'''. The Chronicle of Alberic de Trois-Fontaines refers to the wife of "Sanctio [frater rege Petro de Arragonum" [presumably an error for "Alfonso"] as "neptem…comitis de Forc
2 CONC alcarie"[360]. Rodrigo of Toledo’s De rebus Hispaniæ records that "Aldefonso" married "neptem comitis Folocalquerii"[361]. A Brevis historia comitum Provinciæ records that "Idelfonsus...comes Provinciæ" married "Gersendem neptem c
2 CONC omitis Folocalquerii"[362]. “Garsendis uxor quondam Ildefonsi comitis Provinciæ” donated her rights “in comitatu Forcalqueriensi”, granted by “Guillelmo quondam comite Forcalqueriensi avo meo”, to “Raymundo Berengario filio meo” w
2 CONC ith “filiæ meæ sororis tuæ Garsendis” as substitute should he die, with the consent of “patre meo Raines de Castelar”, by charter dated 30 Nov 1209[363]. 
2 CONT * '''Nun at Celle''' 1222. 
2 CONT * '''m''' (Aix-en-Provence Jul 1193) [https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/PROVENCE.htm#AlfonsoIIdied1209A '''Infante don ALFONSO BERENGUER de Aragón'''], son of ALFONSO II King of Aragon & his wife Infanta doña Sancha de Castilla ([1
2 CONC 180]-Palermo Feb 1209). He succeeded his father in 1195 as ALFONSO II Comte de Provence, Millau & Razès.
2 CONT <nowiki>—————————————————————————————————————————————</nowiki>
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garsenda,_Countess_of_Forcalquier
2 CONT
2 CONT Garsenda or Garsende (II) de Sabran (c. 1180 – c. 1242) was the Countess of Provence as the wife of Alfonso II from 1193 and the Countess of Forcalquier in her own right from 1209. She brought Forcalquier to the House of Barcelona
2 CONC  and united it to Provence. She was also a patron of Occitan literature, especially the troubadours, and herself wrote some lyric poetry and is counted among the trobairitz as Garsenda de Proensa or Proença. She was, in the words 
2 CONC of her most recent editors, "one of the most powerful women in Occitan history".
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life and marriage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Garsenda was the daughter of Renier de Sabran, lord of Caylar and Ansouis, and Garsenda, daughter of William IV of Forcalquier. She was named after her mother, who was the heiress of William IV, but predeceased him. Garsenda there
2 CONC fore inherited Forcalquier from her grandfather. She was only thirteen years of age when, in 1193, her grandfather William IV and Alfonso II signed the Treaty of Aix whereby Garsenda would inherit William's county and would marry 
2 CONC Alfonso, who was in line to become Count of Provence. The marriage took place at Aix-en-Provence in July 1193.  They had at least two children, Raymond Berengar IV and Garsenda, who married Guillermo II de Montcada, and bore him t
2 CONC wo children, including Gaston VII, Viscount of Béarn.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Regency and patronage'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1209 both William IV and Alfonso died and Garsenda became the natural guardian of their heir, her son, Raymond Berengar IV. Initially her brother-in-law, Peter II of Aragon, assigned the regency of Provence to his brother Sanch
2 CONC o, but when Peter died in 1213 Sancho became regent of Aragon and passed Provence and Forcalquier to his son Nuño Sánchez. Dissension broke out between the Catalans and the partisans of the countess, who accused Nuño of attempting
2 CONC  to supplant his nephew in the county. The Provençal aristocracy originally took advantage of the situation for their own ambitious ends, but eventually they lined up behind Garsenda and removed Nuño, who returned to Catalonia. Th
2 CONC e regency was passed to Garsenda and a regency council was established consisting of the native nobles.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was probably during her tenure as regent (1209/1213–1217/1220) that Garsenda became the focus of a literary circle of poets, though the ''vida'' of Elias de Barjols refers to his patron as Alfonso. There is a ''tenso'' between 
2 CONC a ''bona dompna'' (good lady), identified in one chansonnier as ''la contessa de Proessa,'' and an anonymous troubadour. The two ''coblas'' of the exchange are found in two different orders in the two chansonniers, called ''F'' an
2 CONC d ''T'', that preserve them. It cannot be known therefore who spoke first, but the woman's half begins ''Vos q'em semblatz dels corals amadors''.  In the poem the countess declares her love for her interlocutor, who then responds 
2 CONC courteously but carefully. Under some interpretations the troubadour is Gui de Cavaillon, whose ''vida'' repeats the rumour (probably unfounded) that he was the countess' lover. Gui, however, was at the Provençal court between 120
2 CONC 0 and 1209, pushing the date of the exchange forward a bit. Elias de Barjols apparently "fell in love" with her as a widow and wrote songs about her "for the rest of his life," until he entered a monastery. Raimon Vidal also prais
2 CONC ed her renowned patronage of troubadours.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Poetry'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Vos q'em semblatz dels corals amadors, 
2 CONT ja non volgra que fossetz tan doptanz; 
2 CONT e platz me molt quar vos destreing m'amors, 
2 CONT qu'atressi sui eu per vos malananz. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ez avetz dan en vostre vulpillatge 
2 CONT quar no.us ausatz de preiar enardir, 
2 CONT e faitz a vos ez a mi gran dampnatge; 
2 CONT que ges dompna no ausa descobrir 
2 CONT tot so qu'il vol per paor de faillir.  
2 CONT
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT You're so well-suited as a lover, 
2 CONT I wish you wouldn't be so hesitant; 
2 CONT but I'm glad my love makes you the penitent, 
2 CONT otherwise I'd be the one to suffer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Still, in the long run it's you who stands to lose 
2 CONT if you're not brave enough to state your case, 
2 CONT and you'll do both of us great harm if you refuse. 
2 CONT For a lady doesn't dare uncover 
2 CONT her true will, lest those around her think her base.  
2 CONT
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Retirement and later life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1220 Guillaume de Sabran, a nephew of William IV, who claimed Forcalquier and had been in revolt in the region of Sisteron, was neutralised in part through the mediation of the Archbishop of Aix, Bermond le Cornu.  By 1217 or 1
2 CONC 220 Garsenda had finally ceded Forcalquier to her son and handed the reins of government over.
2 CONT
2 CONT Garsenda retired to the monastery of La Celle in 1222 or 1225.  In 1242, she went to visit her newly born great-granddaughter, Beatrice of England, and her parents in Bordeaux. As the father, Henry III of England, was engaged in a
2 CONC  war in France at the time, she brought 60 knights to his service.  Garsend may have been alive as late as 1257, when a certain woman of that name made a donation to a church of St-Jean on the condition that three priests be kept 
2 CONC to pray for her soul and that of her husband.
