2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps03/ps03_415.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ancestral Roots..." (Balt., 1992) 111-29 states he b. 1198. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Weis' "Ancestral Roots. . ." (101:28), (104:28), (111:29), (133:27). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Stuart's "Royalty For Commoners" (54:26) & (164:27). 
2 CONT
2 CONT By Ramon's time Provence had been at peace for two and a half centuries. There were few lands that had enjoyed peace and prosperity with so little interruption. Since the end of the tenth century Provence had grown more in populat
2 CONC ion and wealth than any other part of Europe. Few men in history have been more successful in finding powerful and influential mates for their daughters than Ramon. His four daughters married two sets of brothers - all of them kin
2 CONC gs! Margaret married King Louis IX of France; ELEANOR married KING HENRY III OF ENGLAND; Sanchia married HENRY's brother, Richard of Cornwall, who was recognized for a 
2 CONT
2 CONT time as the German Emporer; and Beatrice, the youngest and his appointed heir, married Louis' brother, Charles of Anjou, who at one time or another held the titles King of Sicily and King of Jerusalem and was 
2 CONT
2 CONT briefly master of most of Italy and Greece. He was almost able to make an independent state of Burgundy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon bequeathed to Beatrice an administrative machine to be rivaled only by those created by the Normans in England and Sicily. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Europaische Stammtafeln ii, 190: 
2 CONT
2 CONT References: [GENSERV],[AR7],[Weis1],[PRES.GED]
