2 CONT     * Wheeler, Bonnie, and Parsons, John Carmi. Eleanor of Aquitaine: Lord and Lady. Palgrave Macmillan, 2002. ISBN 0230602363.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] External links Search Wikimedia Commons 	Wikimedia Commons has media related to: Eleanor of England (1162-1214)
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Adrian Fletcher’s Paradoxplace – Leonora’s Tomb in the Cistercian Nunnery of Santa Maria de Real Huelgas in Burgos, Spain
2 CONT
2 CONT Spanish royalty Preceded by Richeza of Poland 	Queen consort of Castile 1177–1214 	Succeeded by Mafalda of Portugal
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Princesa de Inglaterra y reina consorte de Castilla entre los años 1170 y 1214, por su matrimonio con el rey Alfonso VIII de Castilla. Fue hija del rey Enrique II de Inglaterra y de su esposa, la reina Leonor de Aquitania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fue madre, entre otros, de los reyes Enrique I de Castilla y Berenguela I de Castilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fue hija del rey Enrique II de Inglaterra y de su esposa, la reina Leonor de Aquitania. Por parte paterna fueron sus abuelos Godofredo V de Anjou y su esposa Matilde de Inglaterra, hija del rey Enrique I de Inglaterra. Por parte m
2 CONC aterna fueron sus abuelos Guillermo X de Poitiers, conde de Poitiers, y su esposa Leonor de Châtellerault, duquesa de Aquitania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fueron sus hermanos, entre otros, los reyes Ricardo Corazón de León y Juan sin Tierra, monarcas de Inglaterra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nació en 1160 y en septiembre de 1170 se desposó en la ciudad de Tarazona con Alfonso VIII de Castilla, quien había sido coronado en la ciudad de Burgos un año antes, y antes del matrimonio, sus padres, los reyes de Inglaterra, co
2 CONC ncedieron como dote a su hija el ducado de Aquitania, que pertenecía a su madre, la reina Leonor de Aquitania. Alfonso VIII de Castilla concedió como dote a su esposa los castillos de Burgos y Castrojeriz, Amaya, Avia, Saldaña, Mo
2 CONC nzón de Campos, Carrión de los Condes, Dueñas, Tariego de Cerrato, Cabezón, Medina del Campo, Astudillo, Aguilar y Villaescusa, y las rentas del puerto de Santander, Cabedo, Besgo de Santillana, Tudela, Calahorra, Arnedo, Vigera, 
2 CONC Metria, las del castillo y ciudad de Nájera, Logroño, Grañón, Belorado, Pancorbo, Piedralada, Poza de la Sal, monasterio de Rodilla, Atienza, Ciudad de Osma, Peñafiel, Curiel de Duero, Hita, Zurita y Peñanegra, y para su cámara la
2 CONC  ciudad de Burgos y la villa de Castrojeriz, con todos sus derechos y rentas, y le donaría además la mitad de los territorios que conquistase a los musulmanes desde que su matrimonio fuera celebrado.
2 CONT
2 CONT El matrimonio sirvió para reforzar la frontera pirenaica, y Leonor Plantagenet aportó además como dote de boda el condado de Gascuña que Alfonso VIII nunca pudo anexionar a la corona de Castilla. Sin embargo, numerosos caballeros 
2 CONC gascones vinieron a la península para ayudar a su señor en la lucha contra los almohades. Hacia 1180 el monarca castellano y su esposa decidieron fundar un monasterio de monjas cistercienses, el monasterio de las Huelgas de Burgos
2 CONC . Aquella fundación, dependiente en principio del Monasterio de Santa María de la Caridad de Tulebras, en (Navarra), se convirtió desde 1187, por deseo de los reyes, en casa madre de todas las abadías femeninas cistercienses en te
2 CONC rritorio de Castilla y fue elegido por Alfonso VIII y su esposa como panteón real para ellos y sus descendientes.
2 CONT
2 CONT La reina ordenó edificar en la catedral de Toledo una capilla dedicada a Santo Tomás Becket o Tomás Canturiense (de Canterbury), como era conocido entonces, que fue la primera dedicada al santo británico fuera de las Islas Británi
2 CONC cas. La capilla no se conserva en la actualidad porque fue destruída y su espacio pasó a formar parte de la capilla de Santiago de la Catedral de Toledo, que fue edificada por el condestable Álvaro de Luna para construir su propia
2 CONC  capilla funeraria. Asimismo en 1183, sobre el solar de la mezquita de Cuenca ordenó que se comenzase a construir una catedral para la ciudad manchega, que había sido reconquistada recientemente.
2 CONT
2 CONT La reina Leonor Plantagenet falleció el día 31 de octubre de 1214, varias semanas después de que falleciera su esposo, el rey Alfonso VIII de Castilla, quien falleció el día 6 de octubre de 1214.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sources: See those of her descendants.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Queen Leonora (October 13, 1162 – October 31, 1214), was born as Princess Eleanor of England (and Aquitaine) and became Leonora, Queen of Castile as wife of Alfonso VIII of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in the castle at Domfront, Normandy, and was baptised by Henry of Marcy. She was the sixth child and second daughter of King Henry II of England and his wife Queen Eleanor of Aquitaine. Her godfather was the chronicle
2 CONC r Robert of Torigny, who had a special interest in her and recorded her life as best he could. She received her first name as a namesake of her mother, whose name "Eleanor" (or Alienor) had previously been unrecorded though may ha
2 CONC ve been related to the Greek Helen or the Italian Elena. Another view holds that in the Occitan language, Eleanor simply meant "the other Aenor," since Eleanor of Aquitaine was named for her mother, called Aenor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was a younger maternal half-sister of Marie de Champagne and Alix of France. She was a younger sister of William, Count of Poitiers, Henry the Young King, Matilda of England, Richard I of England and Geoffrey II, Duke of B
2 CONC rittany. She was also an older sister of Joan of Sicily and John of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was eighteen years old, in 1180, she was married to Alfonso VIII. The marriage was arranged to secure the Pyrennean border, with Gascony offered as her dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of all Eleanor of Aquitaine's daughters, her namesake Eleanor (who was called Leonor by her Spanish subjects) best inherited her mother's political influence. She reigned alongside her husband, who specified in his will that she w
2 CONC as to rule alongside their son in the event of his death. It was she who persuaded him to marry their daughter Berenguela to the king of Leon in the interest of peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Alfonso died, his queen was reportedly so devastated with grief that she was unable to preside over the burial. Their daughter Berenguela instead performed these honors. Leonora then took sick and died only twenty-eight days 
2 CONC after her husband, and was buried at Las Huelgas abbey in Burgos.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Leonora and Alfonso Berenguela, Queen of Castile (August 1180 - 8 November 1246), married King Alfonso IX of Leon  Sancho of Castile (born & died 1181)  Sancha of Castile (1182 - 3 February 1184)  Henry of Castile (bor
2 CONC n & died 1184)  Urraca, princess of Castile (1186-1220), married King Alfonso II of Portugal  Blanca of Castile (4 March 1188 - 26 November 1252), married King Louis VIII of France  Fernando of Castile (29 September 1189 - 1211)  
2 CONC Mafalda of Castile (1191-1204)  Constance of Castile (1195-1198)  Leonor of Castile (1200-1244), married King James I of Aragon  Constanza, nun at Las Huelgas (1201-1243)  Henry I, King of Castile (14 April 1204 - 1217)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eleanor of England (also known as Leonora de Ingleterra) was Queen of Castile as wife of Alfonso VIII of Castile. When she was eighteen years old, she was married to Alfonso VIII. The marriage was arranged to secure the Pyrennean 
2 CONC border, with Gascony offered as her dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Alfonso died, his queen was reportedly so devastated with grief that she was unable to preside over the burial. Their daughter Berenguela instead performed these honors. Leonora then took sick and died only twenty-eight days 
2 CONC after her husband.
2 CONT
2 CONT She bore 12 children with Alfonso, including our ancestor Blanche of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonora_of_England for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eleanor of England (later Leonora; 13 October 1162 – 31 October 1214) was Queen of Castile as wife of Alfonso VIII of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in the castle at Domfront, Normandy, and was baptised by Henry of Marcy. She was the sixth child and second daughter of King Henry II of England and his wife Queen Eleanor of Aquitaine. Her godfather was the chronicle
2 CONC r Robert of Torigny, who had a special interest in her and recorded her life as best he could. She received her first name as a namesake of her mother, whose name "Eleanor" (or Alienor) had previously been unrecorded though may ha
2 CONC ve been related to the Greek Helen or the Italian Elena. Another view holds that in the Occitan language, Eleanor simply meant "the other Aenor," since Eleanor of Aquitaine was named for her mother, called Aenor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor was a younger maternal half-sister of Marie de Champagne and Alix of France. She was a younger sister of William IX, Count of Poitiers, Henry the Young King, Matilda, Duchess of Saxony, Richard I of England and Geoffrey II
2 CONC , Duke of Brittany. She was also an older sister of Joan of Sicily and John of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was eighteen years old, in September 1180, she was married to Alfonso VIII. The marriage was arranged to secure the Pyrennean border, with Gascony offered as her dowry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of all Eleanor of Aquitaine's daughters, her namesake Eleanor (who was called Leonor by her Spanish subjects) best inherited her mother's political influence. She reigned alongside her husband, who specified in his will that she w
2 CONC as to rule alongside their son in the event of his death. It was she who persuaded him to marry their daughter Berenguela to the king of Leon in the interest of peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Alfonso died, his queen was reportedly so devastated with grief that she was unable to preside over the burial. Their daughter Berenguela instead performed these honors. Leonora then took sick and died only twenty-eight days 
2 CONC after her husband, and was buried at Las Huelgas abbey in Burgos.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT También conocida como Leonor Plantagenet de Inglaterra y Leonor de Castilla, fue Reina de Castilla (1170-1214).  Casada con Alfonso VIII de Castilla con solamente nueve años de edad, su dote fue el condado de Gascuña (lo que asegu
2 CONC raría el borde de los Pirineos), aunque su marido nunca lo pudo anexionar al reino de Castilla. Tuvo gran influencia política y reinó interviniendo junto al rey castellano, el cual especificó en su testamento que sería ella quien 
2 CONC gobernaría Castilla durante la minoría de edad del heredero.
2 CONT
