2 CONT
2 CONT (plus many more)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche of Castile (March 4, 1188 – November 26, 1252), wife of Louis VIII of France. She was born in Palencia, Spain, the third daughter of Alfonso VIII, king of Castile, and of Eleanor of England. Eleanor was a daughter of Henry
2 CONC  II of England and his Queen consort Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In consequence of a treaty between Philip Augustus and John of England, Blanche's sister Urraca was betrothed to the former's son, Louis. Their grandmother Eleanor, upon getting acquainted with the two sisters, judged that Blanche
2 CONC 's personality was more fit for a queen of France. In the spring of 1200 she brought her to France instead. On May 22, 1200 the treaty was finally signed, John ceding with his niece the fiefs of Issoudun and Gracay, together with 
2 CONC those that André de Chauvigny, lord of Châteauroux, held in Berry, of the English crown. The marriage was celebrated the next day, at Portmort on the right bank of the Seine, in John's domains, as those of Philip lay under an inte
2 CONC rdict.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche first displayed her great qualities in 1216, when Louis, who on the death of John claimed the English crown in her right, invaded England, only to find a united nation against him. Philip Augustus refused to help his son, 
2 CONC and Blanche was his sole support. The queen established herself at Calais and organized two fleets, one of which was commanded by Eustace the Monk, and an army under Robert of Courtenay; but all her resolution and energy were in v
2 CONC ain. Although it would seem that her masterful temper exercised a sensible influence upon her husband's gentler character, her role during his reign (1223-1226) is not well known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death he left Blanche regent and guardian of his children. Of her twelve or thirteen children, six had died, and Louis, the heir – afterwards the sainted Louis IX – was but twelve years old. The situation was critical, fo
2 CONC r the hard-won domains of the house of Capet seemed likely to fall to pieces during a minority. Blanche had to bear the whole burden of affairs alone, to break up a league of the barons (1226), and to repel the attack of the king 
2 CONC of England (1230). But her energy and firmness overcame all dangers. There was an end to the calumnies circulated against her, based on the poetical homage rendered her by Theobald IV of Champagne, and the prolonged stay in Paris 
2 CONC of the papal legate, Romano Bonaventura, cardinal of Sant' Angelo.
2 CONT
2 CONT The nobles were awed by her warlike preparations or won over by adroit diplomacy, and their league was broken up. St Louis owed his realm to his mother, but he himself always remained somewhat under the spell of her imperious pers
2 CONC onality. After he came of age, in 1234, her influence upon him may still be traced. The same year, he was married, and Blanche became Queen mother. Louis IX married Marguerite of Provence, who was the eldest of four daughters of R
2 CONC amon, count of Provence, and Beatrice of Savoy. In 1248 Blanche again became regent, during Louis IX's absence on the crusade, a project which she had strongly opposed. In the disasters which followed she maintained peace, while d
2 CONC raining the land of men and money to aid her son in the East. At last her strength failed her. She fell ill into a bale of hay at Melun in November 1252, and was taken to Paris, but lived only a few days. She was buried at Maubuis
2 CONC son.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 11, in the year 1200, Blanche of Castile (known in Spanish as Blanca de Castilla) married 12-year-old Louis of Artois, who would become King Louis VIII of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche first displayed her great qualities in 1216, when Louis, who on the death of King John claimed the English crown in her right, invaded England, only to find a united nation against him. King Philip Augustus of France refus
2 CONC ed to help his son Louis, and Blanche was his sole support. She established herself at Calais and organized two fleets, one of which was commanded by Eustace the Monk, and an army under Robert of Courtenay; but all her resolution 
2 CONC and energy were in vain. Although it would seem that her masterful temper exercised a sensible influence upon her husband's gentler character, her role during his short reign (1223-1226) is not well known.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Louis's death, Blanche masterfully managed the regency and continued as powerful Queen Mother even when her son Louis IX was old enough to become King. She was again regent when her son went off on another foolish and futile
2 CONC  Crusade. 
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blanche_of_Castile for more information.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In consequence of a treaty between Philip Augustus and John of England, Blanche's sister Urraca was betrothed to the former's son, Louis. Their grandmother Eleanor, upon getting acquainted with the two sisters, judged that Blanche
2 CONC 's personality was more fit for a queen of France. In the spring of 1200 she brought her to France instead. On May 22, 1200 the treaty was finally signed, John ceding with his niece the fiefs of Issoudun and Gracay, together with 
2 CONC those that André de Chauvigny, lord of Châteauroux, held in Berry, of the English crown. The marriage was celebrated the next day, at Portmort on the right bank of the Seine, in John's domains, as those of Philip lay under an inte
2 CONC rdict.
2 CONT
2 CONT Blanche first displayed her great qualities in 1216, when Louis, who on the death of John claimed the English crown in her right, invaded England, only to find a united nation against him. Philip Augustus refused to help his son, 
2 CONC and Blanche was his sole support. The queen established herself at Calais and organized two fleets, one of which was commanded by Eustace the Monk, and an army under Robert of Courtenay; but all her resolution and energy were in v
2 CONC ain. Although it would seem that her masterful temper exercised a sensible influence upon her husband's gentler character, her role during his reign (1223-1226) is not well known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death he left Blanche regent and guardian of his children. Of her twelve or thirteen children, six had died, and Louis, the heir – afterwards the sainted Louis IX – was but twelve years old. The situation was critical, fo
2 CONC r the hard-won domains of the house of Capet seemed likely to fall to pieces during a minority. Blanche had to bear the whole burden of affairs alone, to break up a league of the barons (1226), and to repel the attack of the king 
2 CONC of England (1230). But her energy and firmness overcame all dangers. There was an end to the calumnies circulated against her, based on the poetical homage rendered her by Theobald IV of Champagne, and the prolonged stay in Paris 
2 CONC of the papal legate, Romano Bonaventura, cardinal of Sant' Angelo. The nobles were awed by her warlike preparations or won over by adroit diplomacy, and their league was broken up. St Louis owed his realm to his mother, but he him
2 CONC self always remained somewhat under the spell of her imperious personality. After he came of age, in 1234, her influence upon him may still be traced. The same year, he was married, and Blanche became Queen mother. Louis IX marrie
2 CONC d Marguerite of Provence, who was the eldest of four daughters of Ramon, count of Provence, and Beatrice of Savoy. In 1248 Blanche again became regent, during Louis IX's absence on the crusade, a project which she had strongly opp
2 CONC osed. In the disasters which followed she maintained peace, while draining the land of men and money to aid her son in the East. At last her strength failed her. She fell ill into a bale of hay at Melun in November 1252, and was t
2 CONC aken to Paris, but lived only a few days. She was buried at Maubuisson.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Blanche of Castile (Blanca de Castilla in Spanish; 4 March 1188 – 26 November 1252), wife of Louis VIII of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Palencia, Spain, the third daughter of Alfonso VIII, king of Castile, and of Eleanor of England. Eleanor was a daughter of Henry II of England and his Queen consort Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Life
2 CONT
2 CONT In consequence of a dangerous treaty between Philip Augustus and John of England, Blanche's sister Urraca was betrothed to the former's son, Louis. Their grandmother Eleanor, upon getting acquainted with the two sisters, judged th
2 CONC at Blanche's personality was more fit for a queen of France. In the spring of 1200 she brought her to France instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 22 May 1200 the treaty was finally signed, John ceding with his niece the fiefs of Issoudun and Graçay, together with those that André de Chauvigny, lord of Châteauroux, held in Berry, of the English crown. The marriage was cel
2 CONC ebrated the next day, at Portmort on the right bank of the Seine, in John's domains, as those of Philip lay under an interdict.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the English baron's rebellion 1215-16 against King John, it was Blanche's English ancestry as granddaughter to Henry II that led to Louis being offered the throne of England as Louis I. However, with the death of John in Oc
2 CONC tober 1216, the barons changed their allegiance to John's son, the nine-year-old Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis continued to claim the English crown in her right, only to find a united nation against him. Philip Augustus refused to help his son, and Blanche was his sole support. The queen established herself at Calais and organized tw
2 CONC o fleets, one of which was commanded by Eustace the Monk, and an army under Robert of Courtenay; but all her resolution and energy were in vain. With French forces defeated at Lincoln in May 1217 and then routed on their way back 
2 CONC to their London stronghold, Louis desperately needed the reinforcements from France. On 24 August, the English fleet destroyed the French fleet carrying those reinforcements off Sandwich and Louis was forced to sue for peace.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regency
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death he left Blanche regent and guardian of his children. Of her twelve or thirteen children, six had died, and Louis, the heir — afterwards the sainted Louis IX — was but twelve years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT The situation was critical, for the hard-won domains of the house of Capet seemed likely to fall to pieces during a minority. Blanche had to bear the whole burden of affairs alone, to break up a league of the barons (1226), and to
2 CONC  repel the attack of the king of England (1230). But her energy and firmness overcame all dangers.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was an end to the calumnies circulated against her, based on the poetical homage rendered her by Count Theobald IV of Champagne, a.k.a. King Theobald I of Navarre since 1234, and the prolonged stay in Paris of the papal lega
2 CONC te, Romano Bonaventura, cardinal of Sant' Angelo. The nobles were awed by her warlike preparations or won over by adroit diplomacy, and their league was broken up. St Louis owed his realm to his mother, but he himself always remai
2 CONC ned somewhat under the spell of her imperious personality.
2 CONT
2 CONT She did not have a good relationship with her daughter-in-law Margaret of Provence, perhaps due to the strong relationship she had with her son. Jean de Joinville tells of the time when Queen Margaret was giving birth and Blanche 
2 CONC entered the room telling her son to leave saying "Come ye hence, ye do naught here". Queen Margaret then allegedly fainted out of distress. When Queen Blanche was present in the royal household she did not like Margaret and Louis 
2 CONC to be together "except when he went to lie with her".
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen Mother
2 CONT
2 CONT After Louis came of age, in 1234, aged 20, her influence upon him may still be traced. The same year, he was married, and Blanche became Queen mother. Louis married Margaret of Provence, who was the eldest of four daughters of Ram
2 CONC on, count of Provence, and Beatrice of Savoy. In 1248 Blanche again became regent, during Louis IX's absence on the crusade, a project which she had strongly opposed. In the disasters which followed she maintained peace, while dra
2 CONC ining the land of men and money to aid her son in the East. At last her strength failed her. She fell ill at Melun in November 1252, and was taken to Paris, but lived only a few days. She was buried at Maubuisson and the last surv
2 CONC ing grandchild of Henry II of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine to die.
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides the works of Joinville and William of Nangis, see Élie Berger, "Histoire de Blanche de Castille, reine de France", in Bibliothèque des Ecoles françaises d'Athènes et de Rome, vol. lxx. (Paris, 1895); Le Nain de Tillemont, 
2 CONC "Vie de Saint Louis", ed. by J. de Gaulle for the Société de l'histoire de France (6 vols., 1847–1851); and Paulin Paris, "Nouvelles recherches sur les mœurs de la reine Blanche et de Thibaud", in Cabinet historique (1858).
2 CONT
2 CONT In popular culture
2 CONT
2 CONT An image of Blanche of Castille has been used on the home kit of French Rugby Union team Stade Français since the 2008 season.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT .Margaret of Provence (Forcalquier , Spring 1221 – 21 December 1295, Paris ) was the eldest daughter of Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Provence  and Beatrice of Savoy .
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were Alfonso II, Count of Provence  and Gersende II de Sabran, Countess of Forcalquier . Her maternal grandparents were Thomas I of Savoy  and Marguerite of Geneva , daughter of William I of Geneva  and B
2 CONC eatrice de Faucigny.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her younger sisters were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Provence  became the Queen consort of Henry III of England . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sanchia of Provence  who became the Queen consort of Richard, Earl of Cornwall  and rival King of the Germans . 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatrice of Provence  who was the Queen consort of Charles I of Sicily  
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT On 27 May 1234 at the age of thirteen, Margaret became the Queen consort  of France and wife of Louis IX of France , by whom she had eleven children. She was crowned on the following day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret, like her sisters, was noted for her beauty, she was said to be "pretty with dark hair and fine eyes", and in the early years of their marriage she and Louis enjoyed a warm relationship. Her Franciscan confessor, William 
2 CONC de St. Pathus, related that on cold nights Margaret would place a robe around Louis' shoulders, when her deeply religious husband rose to pray. Another anecdote recorded by St. Pathus related that Margaret felt that Louis' plain c
2 CONC lothing was unbecoming to his royal dignity, to which Louis replied that he would dress as she wished, if she dressed as he wished. However, the chronicler Joinville noted with disapproval that Louis rarely asked after his wife an
2 CONC d children, and in later years Louis became vexed with Margaret's ambition.
2 CONT
2 CONT She accompanied Louis on his first crusade  and was responsible for negotiations and ransom when he was captured. She was thus for a brief time the only woman ever to lead a crusade. During this period, while in Damietta </wiki/Da
2 CONC mietta>, she gave birth to Jean Tristran.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Louis on his second crusade, during which she remained in France, she returned to Provence.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was devoted to her sister Queen Eleanor of England, and they stayed in contact until Eleanor's death in 1291. Margaret herself died four and a half years after her sister, on 21 December 1295. She was seventy-four years old.
