2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Béla III (Hungarian: III. Béla, Croatian: Bela II, Slovak: Belo III), (c. 1148 – 23 April 1196), King of Hungary and Croatia (1172–1196), Duke of Croatia and Dalmatia (1161–1163). He was educated in the Court of the Byzantine Empe
2 CONC ror Manuel I who was planning to ensure his succession in the Byzantine Empire till the birth of his own son. Following his elder brother's death, who had been fighting against the Byzantine Empire, Béla could only ascend to the t
2 CONC hrone with the assistance of the Emperor Manuel I and Pope Alexander III, because a significant part of the Hungarian aristocracy led by his own mother and the Archbishop of Esztergom preferred his younger brother's succession. Bé
2 CONC la was one of the most powerful rulers of Hungary and he was also one of the most wealthy monarchs of Europe of his age. It was probably he who began to organise the Royal Chancellery in Hungary. Marriages and children
2 CONT #1. c. 1170: Agnes of Antioch (1154 – c. 1184), daughter of Raynald of Châtillon, Prince of Antioch and his wife, Constance of Antioch
2 CONT
2 CONT King Emeric of Hungary (1174 – 30 September/November 1204)  Margaret (1175 – after 1223), wife firstly of Emperor Isaac II Angelos, secondly of King Boniface I of Thessalonica and thirdly of Nicolas of Saint-Omer  King Andrew II o
2 CONC f Hungary (c. 1177 – 21 September 1235)  Constance (c. 1180 – 6 December 1240), wife of King Ottokar I of Bohemia 
2 CONT #2. 1185/1186: Marguerite of France (born 1158) (1158 – after 10 September 1197), daughter of King Louis VII of France and his second wife, Constance of Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT =DNA Sequencing
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.geni.com/people/B%C3%A9la-III-of-Hungary/6000000001821102369  Béla III King of Hungary] H1b (mtDNA) R1a (Y-DNA)
2 CONT ''Facial reconstruction of King Béla III of Hungary at an exhibition of the Institute of Hungarian Research (Emese Gábor, 2023)''
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000204484383866&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT A few decades after the collapse of the Avar Khaganate (c. 822 AD), Hungarian invaders conquered the Carpathian Basin (c. 862–895 AD). The first Hungarian ruling dynasty, the Árpáds played an important role in European history dur
2 CONC ing the Middle Ages. King Béla III (1172–1196) was one of the most significant rulers of the dynasty. He also consolidated Hungarian dominance over the Northern Balkans. The provostry church of the Virgin Mary (commonly known as t
2 CONC he Royal Basilica of Székesfehérvár) played a prominent role as a coronation church and burial place of medieval Hungarian kings. The basilica’s building and graves had been destroyed over the centuries. The only royal graves that
2 CONC  remained intact were those of King Béla III and his first spouse, Anna of Antioch. These graves were discovered in 1848. We defined the autosomal STR (short tandem repeat) fingerprints of the royal couple and eight additional ind
2 CONC ividuals (two females and six males) found in the Royal Basilica.[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000204483457823 DNA profiling of Hungarian King Béla III and other skeletal remains originating from the Royal Basili
2 CONC ca of Székesfehérvár]
2 CONT
2 CONT There were three R1a and two R1b statistically predicted Y haplogroups among the male skeletons (Table 3). These are the most frequent and second most frequent haplogroups (25.6 and 18.1% respectively) in the present Hungarian pop
2 CONC ulation (Völgyi et al. 2009). King Béla III was inferred to belong to haplogroup R1a. The R1a Y haplogroup relates paternally to more than 10% of men in a wide geographic area from South Asia to Central Eastern Europe and South Si
2 CONC beria (Underhill et al. 2010). It is the most frequent haplogroup in various populations speaking Slavic, Indo-Iranian, Dravidian, Turkic and Finno-Ugric languages (Underhill et al. 2010).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The inferred mitochondrial haplogroups were H1b for Béla III, H (H1j8 or H1bz) for Anna of Antioch and T2b2b1 for person II/52 (Table 5). The mitochondrial haplogroup H occurs with a frequency of 46% in Europe as a whole (Richards
2 CONC  et al. 2000). H1b is found throughout the area of haplogroup H, but more frequently in Eastern Europe and North Central Europe (7 and 5% of H, respectively) (Loogväli et al. 2004). It is in line with the fact that Béla the Third’
2 CONC s mother was Euphrosyne of Kiev, a daughter of a noblewoman from Novgorod. Haplogroup T2b is frequent throughout Europe, mostly in Western Europe with a frequency of 4.16% (Pala et al. 2012). 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.geni.com/people/Agnes-of-Antioch/6000000007385420123  Anna of Antioch] H1j8 or H1bz (mtDNA) 
2 CONT
