1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raimond-Roger_Trencavel ''Raimond-Roger Trencavel''] (1185 - 10 novembre 1209) est un membre de la Maison Trencavel. Il était vicomte d'Albi, d'Ambialet et de Béziers (fiefs tenus du 
2 CONC comte de Toulouse), et vicomte de Carcassonne et de Razès (fiefs tenus du comte de Barcelone, qui était à la fois roi d'Aragon). Il fut l'un des héros et, en même temps, l'une des premières victimes de la croisade des Albigeois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond Roger Trencavel (Occitan Raimon Rogièr; 1185 – November 10, 1209 AD) was a member of the noble Trencavel family. He was viscount of Béziers and Albi (and thus a vassal of the count of Toulouse), and viscount of Carcassonne
2 CONC  and the Razès (and thus a vassal of the count of Barcelona, which was also ruling Aragon at this time).
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond-Roger was the son of Roger II Trencavel (d. 1194) and of Azalais of Toulouse (also known as the "Countess of Burlats"), daughter of Raymond V of Toulouse and sister of Raymond VI. Raymond-Roger was married to Agnes of Mont
2 CONC pellier. His aunt, Beatrice of Béziers, was the second wife of Raymond VI of Toulouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond-Roger lived in the Château Comtal in the fortified hill town of Carcassonne. His ancestors built the château in the 11th century. Raymond-Roger was not a Cathar, although many of his subjects were. He adopted a laissez-fai
2 CONC re attitude to Catharism – and to other cultures and religions. He relied strongly on Jews to run Béziers, his second seat of power.
2 CONT
2 CONT By mid-1209, at the beginning of the Albigensian Crusade, around 10,000 crusaders had gathered in Lyon and began to march south. In June, Raymond of Toulouse, recognising the potential disaster at hand, promised to act against the
2 CONC  Cathars, and his excommunication was lifted. The crusaders headed towards Montpellier and the lands of Raymond-Roger de Trencavel, aiming for the Cathar communities around Albi and Carcassonne. Like Raymond VI of Toulouse, Raymon
2 CONC d-Roger de Trencavel sought an accommodation with the crusaders, but Raymond-Roger was refused a meeting and raced back to Carcassonne to prepare his defences. The city of Béziers was sacked in July, and its population massacred.
2 CONT
2 CONT The town of Carcassonne was well fortified, but vulnerable and over-populated with refugees. The crusaders, led by a papal legate, Arnaud Amaury, Abbot of Cîteaux, arrived outside the town on August 1, 1209. As vassal of King Pete
2 CONC r II of Aragon, Raymond-Roger had hoped for protection, but Peter was powerless to oppose Pope Innocent III's army and could act only as a mediator.
2 CONT
2 CONT The siege did not last long. By August 7 the crusaders had cut the town's access to water. Raymond-Roger accepted a safe-conduct to negotiate terms of surrender in the Crusader camp. At the conclusion of these negotiations, he was
2 CONC  taken prisoner while still under safe conduct and imprisoned in his own dungeon, where he died, possibly of dysentery, though there were suspicions of poisoning.
2 CONT
2 CONT The town of Carcassonne had surrendered on August 15. The inhabitants were not massacred but were forced to leave the town. Simon de Montfort was granted control of the area encompassing Carcassonne, Albi, and Béziers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raymond-Roger's dispossessed son, Raymond II (1204-1263), formally ceded his rights to Louis IX of France in 1247, after several failed attempts to recover his patrimony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raimond_Roger_Trencavel
