1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Berenguela de León De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berenguela_de_Le%C3%B3n
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela de León (* 1204 - † 12 de abril de 1237). Hija de Alfonso IX de León y de su segunda esposa Berenguela I de Castilla por tanto era hermana de Fernando III el Santo.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1224 contrajo matrimonio en la ciudad de Toledo con el ex rey de Jerusalén Juan de Brienne. Este matrimonio fue impulsado por su madre, Berenguela de Castilla, en una maniobra política que evito que Juan de Brienne desposara a 
2 CONC alguna de las hijas que Alfonso IX tuvo con su primera esposa Teresa de Portugal con lo que evitaba que el rey leonés dispusiera de otro candidato varón que le disputara el trono a Fernando III el Santo.
2 CONT
2 CONT De este matrimonio nacieron cuatro hijos: Alfonso de Acre, Luis de Acre, Juan de Acre y Maria de Brienne.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berenguela_of_Leon
2 CONT
2 CONT Berenguela of Leon (1204 - 12 April 1237) was the third wife but only Empress consort of John of Brienne, Latin Emperor of Constantinople. According to the chronicle of Alberic of Trois-Fontaines, Berenguela was a daughter of Alfo
2 CONC nso IX of León and his second wife Berenguela of Castile. She was a younger sister of Ferdinand III of Castile and Alfonso of Molina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their paternal grandparents were Ferdinand II of León and Urraca of Portugal. Their maternal grandparents were Alfonso VIII of Castile and Leonora of England. Berenguela and John of Brienne had four known children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie of Brienne (1225-1275), who married Emperor Baldwin II of Constantinople.  Alphonso of Brienne (c. 1228-1270), who married Marie d'Issoudon, countess of Eu, and became count of Eu in right of his wife, and was also Great Cha
2 CONC mberlain of France. Died of a plague while involved in the Eighth Crusade.  Jean (John) de Brienne (c. 1230-1296), who in 1258 became Grand Butler of France. Married Marie de Coucy as his first wife. Second wife was Jeanne, daught
2 CONC er of Geoffrey VI, Viscount of Chateaudun.  Louis of Acre (c. 1235-1263), who married Agnes of Beaumont and became Viscount of Beaumont in her right. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berenguela of Leon (1204 - 12 April 1237) was the third wife but only Empress consort of John of Brienne, Latin Emperor of Constantinople.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Berengaria of Leon (1204 - 12 April 1237) was the third wife but only empress consort of John of Brienne, Latin Emperor of Constantinople.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide] 1 Family 2 Marriage 3 Empress 4 Children 5 External links 6 References
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Family According to the chronicle of Alberic of Trois-Fontaines, Berengaria was a daughter of King Alfonso IX of León and Queen Berengaria I of Castile. She was a younger sister of Ferdinand III of Castile and Alfonso of Mo
2 CONC lina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their paternal grandparents were Ferdinand II of León and Urraca of Portugal. Their maternal grandparents were Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage In 1217, Berengaria's brother Ferdinand III had inherited the throne of the Kingdom of Castile through abdication of their mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1223, John of Brienne, aged 53, visited Santiago de Compostela, as a supposed pilgrim. He was by then twice a widower: first, in 1213 of Queen Maria of Jerusalem and then of Stephanie, daughter of Leo II of Armenia, (1150 - kin
2 CONC g of Cilicia in Armenia 1199 - 1219).
2 CONT
2 CONT As a consequence of his visit to Santiago de Compostela, Alfonso IX invited him to marry Sancha and, presumably, through her inherit the Leonese throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT However Berengaria of Castile, a long time divorced and an inheritor in her own right of the Castilian throne, main advisor of her son Ferdinand III, offered one of her own daughters to John instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aging John chose Berengaria of Leon, from Alfonso IX's second doomed marriage, who was a decade younger than her half-sister Sancha, from Alfonso IX's first doomed marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage took place at Toledo in 1224.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Empress In 1229, the throne of the Latin Empire had been inherited by Baldwin II of Courtenay, a twelve-year -old boy. The barons of the Empire decided to secure the safety of the Empire by appointing a regent-Emperor for B
2 CONC aldwin. They chose John who accepted the assignment as a sort of Senior Tutor. In April 1229, John was proclaimed regent at Perugia. They did not arrive at Constantinople until 1231, when John was officially crowned in his new cit
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baldwin II remained the junior co-emperor and only heir to the throne. By agreement, 12 years old Baldwin had been betrothed to around 4 or 5 years old Marie of Brienne, a daughter of John and Berengaria, since 19 April 1229 to fi
2 CONC rmly establish the dynastic alliance of the two co-emperors and the Western Land for Crusades also, Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage did not take place until 1234, when Marie was about nine-years-old and Baldwin about 16.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alberic of Trois-Fontaines records that John died on 27 March 1237, aged around 61. The "Obituaires de Sens Tome" of the Abbey of Maubuisson record that Berenguela died on 12 April 1237, aged around 33, surviving her husband by on
2 CONC ly sixteen days.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is buried in a beautiful marble coffin in the Cathedral of Santiago de Compostela, Spain, but many books and tourist leaflets mention her as if she was Queen Berenguela of Castile, her mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other books however, mention this coffin as being that of another Berengaria (1228–1288) and/or other dates, her niece, daughter of her uncle the King Ferdinand III of Castile, sister of King Alfonso X of Castile. But this niece, 
2 CONC Infanta Berengaria was a nun at the Monastery of las Huelgas, Burgos, of Royal patronage, where Berengaria of Leon's mother, Queen Berenguela of Castile, retired as a former monarch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further, Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England, grandparents of Berengaria of Leon are buried there. Much later, Ferdinand III's and Alfonso X's bodies at Las Huelgas Monastery, near Burgos, would be moved to conquered Se
2 CONC villa's new Cathedral, where they are today.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Children Berengaria and John of Brienne had four known children, known to have been residents in Spain with his cousin, King Alfonso X of Castile, signing widely in many documents for many years with other high nobility wit
2 CONC nesses, Bishops and Archbishops, Royal legitimate and illegitimate offspring and so on :
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Marie of Brienne (1225–1275), who married Emperor Baldwin II of Constantinople. 2.Alphonso of Brienne (c. 1228-1270), who married Marie d'Issoudon, countess of Eu, and became count of Eu in right of his wife, and was also Great 
2 CONC Chamberlain of France. Died of a plague while involved in the Eighth Crusade. 3.John of Brienne (c. 1230-1296), who in 1258 became Grand Butler of France. Married Jeanne, Dame de Chateaudun, daughter of Geoffrey VI, Viscount de Ch
2 CONC ateaudun, as his first wife. His second wife was Marie de Coucy, widow of King Alexander II of Scotland. 4.Louis of Acre (c. 1235-1263), who married Agnes of Beaumont and became Viscount of Beaumont in her right. Royal titles  Pre
2 CONC ceded by Lady of Neuville Latin Empress consort of Constantinople 1229 - 1237 with Marie of Brienne (1234–1237) Succeeded by Marie of Brienne 
2 CONT
