0 @N6000000001353961047@ NOTE Sancho I Garcés (d. 925), king of Pamplona (Navarre) from 905. He expanded his kingdom south of the Ebro River and maintained its independence in spite of the sack of his capital in 924 by the Umayyad caliph 'Abd ar-Rahman III of 
1 CONC Córdoba.
0 @N6000000001354523042@ NOTE Sancho I Garcés (d. 925), king of Pamplona (Navarre) from 905. He expanded his kingdom south of the Ebro River and maintained its independence in spite of the sack of his capital in 924 by the Umayyad caliph 'Abd ar-Rahman III of 
1 CONC Córdoba.
0 @N6000000000945071399@ NOTE Konge av Pamplona (Navarra) 905 - 925. 
1 CONT Navarraslekten var konger av Navarra (Pamplona) fra 810 til 1134. Slekten etterfulgte
1 CONT Castillaslekten som konger av Castilla fra 1026 til 1230 og Leonslekten som konger av Leon
1 CONT fra 1037 til 1230. Deretter var slekten konger av Castilla-Leon fra 1230 til 1516. Sidegrenen
1 CONT var grever av Aragon fra 809 til 922. Aragon ble fra 970 et eget kongerike i union med
1 CONT Navarra, men ble i 1035 skilt ut som eget kongerike under Aragonslekten.
1 CONT Det er usikker hvem som er Sancos foreldre. Mogens Bugge angir i ?Våre forfedre?:
1 CONT Han far, Garcia Jimenéz, var greve av Aragon og døde i 882-84. Han kalles også Garcia
1 CONT Iñigués. Disse navnene er ofte feilaktig tillagt to forskjellige personer. Han var gift med Urraca
1 CONT som var av kongelig byrd og mor til Fortun Garcés, bror til Sancho. Det oppgis at hans farfar,
1 CONT Iñigo Arista, var den første konge av Pamplona og at han døde så sent som i 880.
1 CONT Bent og Vidar Billing Hansen angir i ?Rosensverdslektens forfedre?:
1 CONT Den videre rekkefølge angir tradisjonelt at hans far, Garcia I Iñigues, døde i 880 og at
1 CONT hans farfar, Iñigo Arista, døde i 851 (29 år tidligere enn angitt av Mogens Bugge). Det
1 CONT oppgis at en kilde, ?Nouvelle Larousse illustré? av Claude Augé, angir at hans far var Garcia
1 CONT Jimenez som døde i 883 og at hans farfar var Jimeno Garces, født ca. 800.
1 CONT Sancho etterfulgte sin bror Fortun Garcéz og utvidet sitt territorium til Tudela.
1 CONT I 905 antok han kongenavn, men nedla regjeringen i 919 og gikk i kloster. Landet ble
1 CONT imidlertid i 920 truet av invasjon av maurerne. Han allierte seg med Ordoño III, men de
1 CONT kristne armeer led et fullstendig nederlag ved Valjunquera. Muselmennene trengte helt frem til
1 CONT Frankrike, men led ved tilbaketoget delvis nederlag ved Roncevaux.
1 CONT I 924 foretok sarasenerne en ekspedisjon helt frem til Pamplona som de dog måtte
1 CONT oppgi.
0 @N6000000002322339984@ NOTE He extended his kingdom south of the Ebro River and maintained its
1 CONT independence despite the sacking of his capital in 924 by 'Abd ar-Rahman III.
1 CONT This is the end of this line of Kings of Navarre.
0 @N6000000002187750604@ NOTE Sancho I of Pamplona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho I Garcés (c. 860 – December 11, 925) was king of Pamplona from 905 to 925. He was a son of García Jiménez, who was regent of Pamplona from 870 to 880 while Fortún Garcés was held captive and apparently co-king of Pamplona f
1 CONC rom 860 to his death in 882, and Dadildis de Pallars. Sancho may also have been a co-king during the reign of Fortún. The elderly Sancho (already 45 years old) succeeded King Fortún when the latter was deposed by a coalition of en
1 CONC emies: the Banu Qasi under Lope ibn Mohammed, King Alfonso III of Asturias, and Sancho's uncle, the count of Pallars.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho's first wife Urraca, daughter of the count of Aragón, did not have surviving children. So Sancho married a second time to Toda Aznárez in his old age. His children were thus minors upon his death. Queen Toda (born Teuda de 
1 CONC Larraun) was a daughter of Aznar Sánchez, lord of Larraun, and Oneca Fortúnez, who herself was a daughter of King Fortún. Thus, Toda's children were also descendants of the Arista dynasty of Navarrese monarchs. Sancho fought again
1 CONC st the Moors with repeated success and joined Ultra-Puertos, or Basse-Navarre (Baja Navarra), to his own dominions, also extending his territory as far as Nájera. As a thanksgiving offering for his victories, he founded, in 924, t
1 CONC he convent of Albelda. Before his death, all the Moors had been driven from the country.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho was succeeded by his son García Sánchez, with his widow Toda and brother Jimeno (who used the royal title) acting as regents.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Codex of Roda gives Sancho and Toda six children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Oneca (d.931), married Alfonso IV the Monk of León in 926 
1 CONT Sancha, married firstly Ordoño II of León, secondly Count Alvaro Herraméliz of Álava, and thirdly Fernán González, Count of Castile 
1 CONT Urraca, married Ramiro II of León 
1 CONT Velasquita (or Belasquita), married firstly Munio, count of Vizcaya, secondly Galindo, son of Bernard count of Ribagorza. 
1 CONT Orbita 
1 CONT García, king of Pamplona, married firstly Andregota Galíndez and secondly Teresa 
1 CONT Sancho also had an illegitimate daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Lupa, mother of Raymond I, Count of Bigorre 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Fortun I King of Pamplona
1 CONT 905–925 Succeeded by
1 CONT Jimeno II 
1 CONT Succeeded by
1 CONT García III 
0 @N6000000002240829984@ NOTE He extended his kingdom south of the Ebro River and maintained its
1 CONT independence despite the sacking of his capital in 924 by 'Abd ar-Rahman III.
1 CONT This is the end of this line of Kings of Navarre.
