0 @N6000000002187750502@ NOTE Sancho II of Pamplona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho II Garcés Abarca (after 935 – December 994) was the Jiménez King of Pamplona and Count of Aragon from 970 until his death. He was the son of García III and Andregota, daughter and heiress of Galindo II Aznárez, Count of Ara
1 CONC gon. After his succession, he recognised his younger brother Ramiro as King of Viguera.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Historia General de Navarra by Jaime del Burgo says (referencing in turn the Anales del Reino de Navarra of José de Moret) that on the occasion of the donation of the villa of Alastue by Sancho to the monastery of San Juan de 
1 CONC la Peña in 987, he titled himself "King of Navarre," the first time that title had been used. In other places, he appears as the first King of Aragon and in others the third. These titles, however, did not come into common usage u
1 CONC ntil the late eleventh century. The epithet "Abarca," meaning "sandal," is not contemporary, but is medieval.
1 CONT
1 CONT Under Sancho and his immediate successors, Navarre reached the height of its power and its largest size. During this period, the Navarre was united to the Kingdom of León and the County of Castile by familial bonds. The Navarrese 
1 CONC monarchy supported the young Ramiro II when he secured the throne of León.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon the death of the Caliph of Cordoba, Al-Hakam II, in 976, and the succession of his son Hisham II, who had been taught by Al-Mansur Ibn Abi Aamir, the prospects of the Christian kingdoms seemed dim. The troops of Al-Mansur def
1 CONC eated the Christians at Torrevicente, south of Soria. Afterwards, the Muslims returned to triumph at Taracueña, near Osma. In 975, Sancho was defeated by the Moors at San Esteban de Gormaz, and in 981 at Rueda, a dozen kilometers 
1 CONC from Tordesillas, the Christians suffered another humiliating defeat.
1 CONT
1 CONT Because he could not defeat Al-Mansur by arms, Sancho went to Córdoba as an ambassador for his own kingdom, bringing many gifts for the victorious Al-Mansur, making a pact with him and agreeing to give the Muslim his daughter Urra
1 CONC ca in marriage. From this union was born Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo, the second successor of Al-Mansur who tried to usurp the Caliphate of Córdoba from the Umayyad heir.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 972, he founded the monastery of San Andrés de Cirueña. In 976, at the monastery of Albelda, the cultural and intellectual centre of his kingdom, the Codex Vigilanus was completed. It is one of the most important illuminated ma
1 CONC nuscripts of medieval Spain, containing the canons of the Councils of Toledo, a copy of the Liber Iudiciorum, and the first Western representation of the Arabic numerals, among many other texts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho married Urraca, the daughter of the Castilian count Fernán González and Sancha, Sancho's aunt. The marriage occurred after 962 and before 970. Before 950, Urraca had been married twice previously, to Ordoño III and Ordoño I
1 CONC V of León, from both of whom she separated. Sancho was her third and last husband. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT García IV of Pamplona 
1 CONT Ramiro (died 992) 
1 CONT Gonzalo, was given the county of Aragon under the regency of his mother 
1 CONT Urraca (Abda) the Basque, given to Al-Mansur before entering a convent 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT García III King of Pamplona
1 CONT 970–994 Succeeded by
1 CONT García IV 
1 CONT Count of Aragon
1 CONT 970–994 
