0 @N6000000002187755349@ NOTE García V of Navarre
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT García V of Nájera (in Spanish, García V El de Nájera), was king of Navarre from 1035 to 1054. He was the eldest legitimate son and heir of Sancho the Great, thus he succeeded in his father's inheritance of Navarre. He not only re
1 CONC ceived the patrimony of his family, he was given a seniority amongst his brothers, a sort of "High Kingship". However, his father divided his many conquests among García's brothers: Ramiro, the eldest but illegitimate son, receive
1 CONC d the petty kingdom of Aragón; Ferdinand, the second eldest legitimate son, received Castile (which his father received through marriage to his mother); and his youngest surviving son (legitimate), Gonzalo, received the kingdoms o
1 CONC f Sobrarbe and Ribagorza.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1037, Ferdinand requested García's aid against his brother-in-law, Bermudo III of León, in battle near Pisuerga. The two brothers defeated Bermudo, who died in battle, the final descendant of Pedro de Cantabria, and Ferdinand s
1 CONC ucceeded in León.
1 CONT
1 CONT By aiding Ferdinand, García received his brother's favour and, in a repartition of Castile, he expanded Navarre to the bay of Santander and incorporating the entire Basque Country.
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon he was confronted by his brother Ramiro at Tafalla (1043) and defeated him.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was one of the Christian kings to profit greatly from the weakened taifa kingdoms inhabiting the "vacuum" that was the Caliphate of Córdoba. In 1045, he conquered Calahorra.
1 CONT
1 CONT Relations eventually soured with Ferdinand and war broke out between the fraternal kingdoms, García dying in the Battle of Atapuerca, 15 September, 1054. His nickname comes from his foundation of the monastery of Santa María la Re
1 CONC al in Nájera.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT He was married, in 1038, to Estefanía, daughter of Ramon Borrell, Count of Barcelona, an hereditary count of Barcelona (her dowry was the Cameros), and they produced nine children (four sons, five daughters):
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho "El de Peñalén", king of Navarre, married Placencia 
1 CONT Ramiro (d.1083), lord of Calahorra, married Teresa 
1 CONT Ferdinand, lord of Bucesta, married Nuña de Vizcaya 
1 CONT Raymond the Fraticide (Ramón el Fratricida), lord of Murillo and Cameros 
1 CONT Ermesinda, married Fortún Sánchez de Yarnoz 
1 CONT Mayor, married Guy II of Masón. 
1 CONT Urraca (d.1108), married García Ordóñez 
1 CONT Jimena 
1 CONT Mencia (d.1106), married Lope de Nájera 
1 CONT He also had illegitimate sons:
1 CONT
1 CONT Sancho, lord of Uncastillo and Sangüesa, married Constanza, grandfather of García VI Ramírez, king of Navarre 
1 CONT Ramiro 
1 CONT After García's death, Estefanía is said to have remarried to Roger de Tosny, a Norman adventurer. Estefanía may have been a widow at the time of her marriage to García. A traditional poem tells of the marriage of an illegitimate s
1 CONC on of García (presumed to be Sancho) to his step-sister, a daughter of Estefanía by a former husband.
1 CONT
1 CONT He is sometimes numbered García III or García IV, depending on whether the patronymics, (e.g. Sánchez), are taken into account, whether one counts regents or just monarchs, and whether separate numbering is used for the Íñiguez an
1 CONC d Jiménez dynasties.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sancho III King of Navarre
1 CONT 1035–1054 Sancho IV 
0 @N6000000002176683332@ NOTE The eldest son of Sancho III and grandson of Garcia III, he repelled an
1 CONT attack at Tafallo by his brother Ramiro I of Aragon in 1043. He was invaded
1 CONT by another brother Ferdinand I of Castile to recover annexed territories in
1 CONT 1054 - killed at the Battle of Atapuerca, succeeded by son Sancho IV.
