0 @N6000000000945059173@ NOTE Raimond ?Stryhode? var greve av Barcelona 1076 - 1082. 
1 CONT Han var greve av Barcelona fra 1076 sammen med sin bror, Berengar Raimund II
1 CONT (Fratricida), og lå hele tiden i strid med ham om herredømmet over deres land.
1 CONT Raimond ble myrdet i en skog mellom San Celoni og Hostalrich. Broren ble så, som det
1 CONT synes, eneherre i grevskapet fra 1082 til 1097.
0 @N6000000002187721014@ NOTE Berenguer Ramon II, Count of Barcelona
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT (Redirected from Berenguer Ramon II of Barcelona)
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Berenguer Ramon II the Fratricide (1050s - 1090s) was Count of Barcelona (1076-1097). He was the son of Ramon Berenguer I, and initially ruled jointly with his twin brother Ramon Berenguer II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in 1053 or 1054 he succeeded his father Ramon Berenguer I the Old to co-rule with his twin brother Ramon Berenguer, in 1075. The twins failed to agree and divided their possessions between them, against the will of their late
1 CONC  father. Ramon Berenguer II was killed while hunting in the woods on December 5, 1082. His brother, who went on to become the sole ruler of Catalonia, was credited by popular opinion with having orchestrated this murder.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 1080s Berenguer Ramon's involvement in the internal strife in the Moorish taifa kingdoms brought him in conflict with Cid. In the ensuing war the Count of Barcelona was twice taken prisoner.
1 CONT
1 CONT His life in the 1090s is obscure. Some Catalan nobles are said to have accused him of his brother's assassination before King Alfonso VI of Castile. It is also said that his guilt was determined by trial by combat, which he lost. 
1 CONC After that he went to Jerusalem, either on pilgrimage or as part of the First Crusade, and perished there between 1097 and 1099. Berenguer Ramon II was succeeded by his nephew Ramon Berenguer III, son of Ramon Berenguer II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer I Count of Barcelona
1 CONT with Ramon Berenguer II and Ramon Berenguer III Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer III 
0 @N6000000003051273563@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Ramon Berenguer II, Count of Barcelona", retrieved 15 Oct 07]
1 CONT Ramon Berenguer II the Towhead (1053 or 1054 - December 5, 1082) was Count of Barcelona from 1076 until his death. He ruled jointly with his twin brother Berenguer Ramon II.
1 CONT
1 CONT He succeeded his father Ramon Berenguer I, Count of Barcelona to co-rule with his twin brother Berenguer Ramon, in 1075.
1 CONT
1 CONT The twins failed to agree and divided their possessions between them, against the will of their late father. Ramon Berenguer the Towhead, called so because of the thickness and colour of his hair, was killed while hunting in the w
1 CONC oods in 1082. His brother, who went on to become the sole ruler of Catalonia, was credited by popular opinion of having orchestrated this murder. Berenguer Ramon the Fratricide was later succeeded by Ramon Berenguer's son Ramon Be
1 CONC renguer III.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ramon Berenguers's marriages and descendants
1 CONT - First wife, Aimeris of Narbonne
1 CONT - Second wife, Mahalta (or Maud) of Apulia, born ca. 1059, died 1111/1112, daughter of Duke Robert Guiscard and of Sikelgaita de Salerno
1 CONT - Ramon Berenguer III the Great, count of Barcelona and Provence (before 1082-1131)
0 @N6000000003949866993@ NOTE byname RAMON BERENGUER THE TOWHEAD, Catalan RAMON BERENGUER CAP D'ESTOPES (b. c. 1053--d. Dec. 5, 1082, between Barcelona and Gerona, Catalonia), count of Barcelona who reigned jointly with his twin brother, Berenguer Ramon II, fr
1 CONC om 1076 to 1082.  Following up on the policies of their father, Ramon Berenguer I, they proceeded to build the defenses and repopulate the lands that he had conquered. In 1082, in a forest en route to Gerona, Ramon Berenguer II wa
1 CONC s murdered, probably by his brother, thereafter nicknamed "the Fratricide" ("el Fratricida"). Berenguer Ramon II, now sole master, took part in the civil wars of the Spanish Muslims and reconquered the Tarragona area (1091). He re
1 CONC igned until 1097, when, having lost a trial by combat that was intended to decide the question of his guilt in the crime attributed to him, he went on crusade to Jerusalem, where he died, probably in either 1097 or 1099. [Encyclop
1 CONC aedia Britannica CD '97]
