0 @N6000000003051273393@ NOTE [Wikipedia, "Peter II of Aragon", retrieved 16 Oct 07]
1 CONT Peter II of Aragon (1174 - September 12, 1213), surnamed the Catholic, was the king of Aragon (as Pedro II) and count of Barcelona (as Pere I) from 1196 to 1213.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Alfonso II of Aragon and Sancha of Castile. In 1205 he acknowledged the feudal supremacy of the Papacy and was crowned in Rome by Pope Innocent III, swearing to defend the Catholic faith (hence his surname, "the 
1 CONC Catholic"). He was the first king of Aragon to be crowned by the Pope.
1 CONT
1 CONT On June 15, 1204 he married (as her third husband) Marie of Montpellier, daughter and heiress of William VIII of Montpellier by Eudocia Comnena. She gave him a son, James, but Peter soon discarded her. Marie was popularly venerate
1 CONC d as a saint for her piety and marital suffering, but was never canonized; she died in Rome in 1213.
1 CONT
1 CONT He led the Christian forces to defeat the Moors at the Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa in 1212.
1 CONT
1 CONT Peter returned from Las Navas in autumn 1212 to find that Simon de Montfort had conquered Toulouse, exiling Count Raymond VI of Toulouse, who was Peter's brother-in-law and vassal. Peter crossed the Pyrenees and arrived at Muret i
1 CONC n September 1213 to confront Montfort's army. He was accompanied by Raymond of Toulouse, who tried to persuade Peter to avoid battle and instead starve out Montfort's forces. This suggestion was rejected.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Battle of Muret began on September 12, 1213. The Aragonese forces were disorganized and disintegrated under the assault of Montfort's squadrons. Peter himself was caught in the thick of fighting, and died as a result of a fool
1 CONC hardy act of bravado. He was thrown to the ground and killed. The Aragonese forces broke in panic when their king was slain and the crusaders of Montfort won the day.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon Peter's death the kingdom passed to his only son by Marie of Montpellier, the future James the Conqueror.
0 @N6000000003949866947@ NOTE king of Aragon (1174-1213), son of Alphonso II., and Sancia, daughter of Alphonso VIII. of Castile, had a very marked and curious personal character. As a Spanish prince, he was a crusader, and took a distinguished part in the vic
1 CONC tory over the Almohades at the Navas de Tolosa in 1212. His lands to the north of the Pyrenees brought him in touch with the Albigenses and in 1213 he fought against Simon de Montfort's crusaders, moved not by sympathy with the Al
1 CONC bigenses, but by hostility to the conquering intervention of the north under pretence of religious zeal. A favourer of the troubadours, he combined great personal valour with the most lax morality. He was killed at the battle of M
1 CONC uret (Sept. 12, 1213). [Encyclopædia Britannica, 1961 ed., Vol. 17, p. 639, PETER II]
1 CONT Byname PETER THE CATHOLIC, Spanish PEDRO EL CATÓLICO (b. 1174--d. Sept. 12, 1213, Muret, Fr.), king of Aragon from 1196 to 1213, the eldest son and successor of Alfonso II.
1 CONT Peter married (1204) Mary, lady of Montpellier, and thus greatly extended Aragonese power in southern France. Despite the violent objections of his subjects, he had himself crowned by Pope Innocent III in Rome and declared his kin
1 CONC gdom a feudatory of the Holy See (1204). Peter, with other Spanish kings, took a prominent part in the victory over the Moors at Las Navas de Tolosa (July 16, 1212); but he then went to support his brother-in-law Raymond VI of Tou
1 CONC louse against the crusader Simon de Montfort in Languedoc. . His son James I succeeded him. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97]
0 @N6000000004533182100@ NOTE Notes for Pedro II Rey de Aragón:
1 CONT
1 CONT Peter II, king of Aragon (1174-1213), son of Alphonso II., and Sancia,daughter of Alphonso VIII. of Castile, had a very marked and curiouspersonal character. As a Spanish prince, he was a crusader, and took adistinguished part in 
1 CONC the victory over the Almohades at the Navas deTolosa in 1212. His lands to the north of the Pyrenees brought him intouch with the Albigenses and in 1213 he fought against Simon deMontfort's crusaders, moved not by sympathy with th
1 CONC e Albigenses, butby hostility to the conquering intervention of the north underpretence of religious zeal. A favourer of the troubadours, he combinedgreat personal valour with the most lax morality. He was killed at thebattle of M
1 CONC uret (Sept. 12, 1213). [Encyclopædia Britannica, 1961 ed.,Vol. 17, p. 639, PETER II]; "el Catolico"
1 CONT
1 CONT "Pedro took a ... measure of precaution which aroused muchdissatisfaction among his subjects; he acknowledged the feudalsupremacy of the Papacy and was crowned at Rome by Innocent III in1204, undertaking to defend the Catholic fai
1 CONC th, to respect theprivileges and immunities of the Church and to fight against heresy;he also promised that his kingdom should pay an annual tribute to thePapacy as a feudal vassal, in return for which the Pope and hissuccessors w
1 CONC ere to defend Pedro and his subjects with their apostolicauthority. Pedro received or assumed the title of Católico, but hisaction was greeted with unanimous disfavour by both his Aragonese andCatalan subjects, who declined to rat
1 CONC ify it."
1 CONT
1 CONT http://libro.uca.edu/chaytor/achistory.htm Chapter 4
1 CONT
1 CONT "A History of Aragon and Catalonia" ; H. J. Chaytor
