0 @N6000000002187755197@ NOTE Alfonso VIII of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso VIII (11 November 1155 – 5 October 1214), called the Noble or Él de las Navas, was the King of Castile from 1158 to his death. He is most remembered for his part in the Reconquista and the downfall of the Almohad Caliphate
1 CONC . After having suffered a great defeat with his own army at Alarcos against the Almohads, he led the coalition of Christian princes and foreign crusaders who broke the power of the Almohads in the Battle of the Navas de Tolosa in 
1 CONC 1212, an event which marked the arrival of an irreversible tide of Christian supremacy on the Iberian peninsula.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign saw the domination of Castile over León and, by his alliance with Aragon, he drew those two spheres of Christian Iberia into close connection.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Regency and civil war 
1 CONT 2 Reconquista 
1 CONT 3 Cultural legacy 
1 CONT 4 Children 
1 CONT 5 Notes 
1 CONT 6 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Regency and civil war
1 CONT Alfonso was born to Sancho III of Castile and Blanca, daughter of García VI of Navarre, in Soria on 11 November 1155. He was named after his grandfather Alfonso VII. His early life resembled that of other medieval kings. His fathe
1 CONC r died in 1158 when his mother was also dead. Though proclaimed king when only three years of age, he was regarded as a mere name by the unruly nobles to whom a minority was convenient. Immediately, Castile was plunged into confli
1 CONC cts between the various noble houses vying for ascendancy in the inevitable regency. The Lara and Castro both claimed the regency, as did the boy's uncle, Ferdinand II of León. The devotion of a squire of his household, who carrie
1 CONC d him on the pommel of his saddle to the stronghold of San Esteban de Gormaz, saved him from falling into the hands of the contending factions.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was put in the custody of the loyal village Ávila. At barely fifteen, he came forth to do a man's work by restoring his kingdom to order. It was only by a surprise that he recovered his capital Toledo from the hands of the Lara
1 CONC s.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reconquista
1 CONT In 1174, he ceded Uclés to the Order of Santiago and afterwards this became the order's principal seat. From Uclés, he began a campaign which culminated in the reconquest of Cuenca in 1177. The city surrendered on 21 September, th
1 CONC e feast of Saint Matthew, ever afterwards celebrated by the citizens of the town.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso took the initiative to ally all the major Christian kingdoms of the peninsula — Navarre, León, Portugal, and Aragon — against the Almohads. By the Treaty of Cazorla of 1179, the zones of expansion of each kingdom were defi
1 CONC ned.
1 CONT
1 CONT After founding Plasencia (Cáceres) in 1186, he embarked on a major initiative to unite the Castilian nobility around the Reconquista. In that year, he recuperated part of La Rioja from the Kingdom of Navarre.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1195, after the treaty with the Almohads was broken, he came to the defence of Alarcos on the river Guadiana, then the principal Castilian town in the region. At the subsequent Battle of Alarcos, he was roundly defeated by the 
1 CONC caliph Abu Yaqub Yusuf al-Mansur. The reoccupation of the surrounding territory by the Almohads was quickly commenced with Calatrava falling first. For the next seventeen years, the frontier between Moor and Castilian was fixed in
1 CONC  the hill country just outside Toledo.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally, in 1212, through the mediation of Pope Innocent III, a crusade was called against the Almohads. Castilians under Alfonso, Aragonese and Catalans under Peter II, Navarrese under Sancho VII, and Franks under the archbishop 
1 CONC Arnold of Narbonne all flocked to the effort. The military orders also lent their support. Calatrava first, then Alarcos, and finally Benavente were captured before a final battle was fought at Las Navas de Tolosa near Santa Elena
1 CONC  on 16 July. The caliph Muhammad an-Nasir was routed and Almohad power broken.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Cultural legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT Statue of Alfonso VIII at the Sabatini Gardens in Madrid (J. Villanueva, 1753).Alfonso was the founder of the first Spanish university, a studium generale at Palencia, which, however, did not survive him. His court also served as 
1 CONC an important instrument for Spanish cultural achievement. His marriage (Burgos, September 1170) with Eleanor (Leonora), daughter of Henry II of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine, brought him under the influence of the greatest gove
1 CONC rning intellect of his time. Troubadours and sages were always present, largely due to the influence of Eleanor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso died at Gutierre-Muñoz and was succeeded by his eldest surviving son, Henry I, named after his maternal grandfather.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alfonso was the subject for Lion Feuchtwanger's novel Die Jüdin von Toledo (The Jewess of Toledo), in which is narrated an affair with a Jewish subject in medieval Toledo in a time when Spain was known to be the land of tolerance 
1 CONC and learning for Jews, Christians, and Muslims. The titular Jewish woman of the novel is based on Alfonso's historical paramour, Rahel la Fermosa.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT With Eleanor, he had 12 children:
1 CONT
1 CONT Berenguela, or Berengaria, (August 1180 – 8 November 1246), married Alfonso IX of Leon 
1 CONT Sancho (1181) 
1 CONT Sancha (1182 – 3 February 1184) 
1 CONT Henry (1184) 
1 CONT Urraca (1186 – 1220), married Alfonso II of Portugal 
1 CONT Blanch (4 March 1188 – 26 November 1252), married Louis VIII of France 
1 CONT Ferdinand (29 September 1189 – 1211), on whose behalf Diego of Acebo and the future Saint Dominic travelled to Denmark in 1203 to secure a bride[
1 CONT Mafalda (1191 – 1204) 
1 CONT Constance (1195 – 1198) 
1 CONT Eleanor (1200 – 1244), married James I of Aragon 
1 CONT Constance (1201 – 1243), abbess of Las Huelgas 
1 CONT Henry I (14 April 1204 – 1217), successor 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ Vicaire. pp 89–98. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT COSTA, Ricardo da. "Love and Crime, Chastisement and Redemption in Glory in the Crusade of Reconquest: Alfonso VIII of Castile in the battles of Alarcos (1195) and Las Navas de Tolosa (1212)". In: OLIVEIRA, Marco A. M. de (org.). 
1 CONC Guerras e Imigrações. Campo Grande: Editora da UFMS, 2004, p. 73-94 (ISBN 85-7613-023-8). 
1 CONT Vicaire, M.-H. "Une ambassade dans les Marches," in Pierre Mandonnet, Saint Dominique: l'idée, l'homme et l'oeuvre Vol. 1. Desclée De Brouwer: Paris, 1938. 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Sancho III King of Castile
1 CONT 1158–1214 Succeeded by
1 CONT Henry I 
