1 CONC . After having suffered a great defeat with his own army at Alarcos against the Almohads, he led the coalition of Christian princes and foreign crusaders who broke the power of the Almohads in the Battle of the Navas de Tolosa in 
1 CONC 1212, an event which marked the arrival of an irreversible tide of Christian supremacy on the Iberian peninsula.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign saw the domination of Castile over León and, by his alliance with Aragon, he drew those two spheres of Christian Iberia into close connection.
1 CONT
1 CONT Regency and civil war
1 CONT Alfonso was born to Sancho III of Castile and Blanca of Navarre, daughter of García VI of Navarre, in Soria on 11 November 1155. He was named after his grandfather Alfonso VII. His early life resembled that of other medieval kings
1 CONC . His father died in 1158 when his mother was also dead. Though proclaimed king when only three years of age, he was regarded as a mere name by the unruly nobles to whom a minority was convenient. Immediately, Castile was plunged 
1 CONC into conflicts between the various noble houses vying for ascendancy in the inevitable regency. The Lara and Castro both claimed the regency, as did the boy's uncle, Ferdinand II of León. The devotion of a squire of his household,
1 CONC  who carried him on the pommel of his saddle to the stronghold of San Esteban de Gormaz, saved him from falling into the hands of the contending factions.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was put in the custody of the loyal village Ávila. At barely fifteen, he came forth to do a man's work by restoring his kingdom to order. It was only by a surprise that he recovered his capital Toledo from the hands of the Lara
1 CONC s.
