0 @N6000000002322288256@ NOTE Louis continued his father's pacification program by building the prestigue
1 CONT of the kingship through administrative governmnet based on trustworthy men
1 CONT of humble origin and by consolidating his rule over his royal domains rather
1 CONT than adding new acquisitions.He repudiated his wife Eleanor for misconduct on
1 CONT Mar-21-1152, even though she bore him 2 daughters, thereby losing control of
1 CONT Aquitaine to her new husband, and Louis's ongoing enemy, Henry II of
1 CONT England. With Henry, she bore him 5 sons and 3 daughters of note.
0 @N6000000002134187739@ NOTE Louis VII (Louis the Young), c. 1120?1180, king of France (1137?80), son and successor of King Louis VI. Before his accession he married Eleanor of Aquitaine. A controversy with Pope Innocent II over Louis's refusal to accept the 
1 CONC papal appointee to the archbishopric of Bourges led to a papal interdict on Louis and to warfare between the king and the count of Champagne, who supported the papal candidate. It was settled, after the intervention of St. Bernard
1 CONC  of Clairvaux, by Louis's capitulation (1144) to Pope Celestine II, Innocent's successor. In the course of that war Geoffrey IV (Geoffrey Plantagenet), count of Anjou, completed his conquest of Normandy; Louis, in return for a sma
1 CONC ll concession, acquiesced in the conquest. In 1147, Louis left on the Second Crusade (see Crusades), after appointing Abbé Suger as regent. The crusade failed, and he returned in 1149. In 1152 Louis, suspecting Eleanor of being un
1 CONC faithful, had his marriage with her annulled. Her subsequent marriage with Henry Plantagenet (later King Henry II of England), Geoffrey's son, resulted in Henry's claims to Aquitaine and precipitated recurrent warfare between Loui
1 CONC s and Henry. Louis supported Thomas à Becket during his exile from England and joined in the revolt of Henry's sons (1173?74), but won no territory. He completed his father's work of subduing the barons on the royal domain and con
1 CONC tinued to increase his influence over more distant vassals. His son Philip II succeeded him
