0 @N6000000002187840385@ NOTE Eudes III, Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Eudes III of Burgundy (1166 – July 6, 1218) was duke of Burgundy between 1192 and 1218. Eudes was the eldest son of duke Hugh III and Beatrice d'Albon. He was married twice, first to Teresa, princess of Portugal, daughter of king 
1 CONC Afonso I of Portugal, then to Alice of Vergy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eudes did not follow his father's aggressive policies towards France and proved a worthy ally of king Philip II of France on his wars against John Lackland and the Holy Roman Emperor Otto IV of Germany. He fought bravely against t
1 CONC he latest in the battle of Bouvines, where he lost, according to contemporary chroniclers, two horses beneath him. Eudes was also an important figure in the Crusade against the Cathars. When Philip II refused to get involved, the 
1 CONC Duke of Burgundy stepped forward with the support of the local bishops and his vassals and organized the campaign of 1209 against the Cathar strongholds.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Burgundy family tree
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Hugh III Duke of Burgundy
1 CONT 1192–1218 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Hugh IV 
0 @N6000000005076731123@ NOTE Duc de Bourgogne
0 @N6000000009304980075@ NOTE #Générale#Eudes III, duc de Bourgogne, confirma à l'abbaye S. Benigne de Dijon, dès l'année 1193, son frère Alexandre présent, les privilèges accordés à ce monastère par son père Hugues, mort en Terre Sainte. Il se croisa en 1209 
1 CONC pour faire la guerre aux hérétiques Albigeois, accompagna le roi Philippe Auguste au voyage de Picardie et commanda l'avant-garde de l'armée à la bataille de Bouvines, où il se signala, ayant eu son cheval tué sous lui, et comme i
1 CONC l était extrêmement gros et pesant, il y couru grand risque. Il fonda l'hopital de St. Esprit, aux fauxbourgs de Dijon et mourut à Lyon le 6 Juillet 1218, s'en allant à la croisade outremer. Il est enterré dans l'Eglise de Citeaux
1 CONC , auprès de son père.
0 @N6000000002322298274@ NOTE In 1191, Holy Roman Emperor Henry VI made an attempt to gain the throne of
1 CONT Sicily, his campaign partly financed by ranson paid for Richard the
1 CONT Lion-Heart who had been held prisoner in Germany. Before reaching Sicily,
1 CONT Tancred died suddenly leaving only a young son, William III. The barons
1 CONT rallied to Henry, who was crowned on Christmas day, 1194. Tancred's family
1 CONT was promised safe conduct but was seized  and made captive in Germany, and
1 CONT William was likely murdered there, ending the 125-year Norman rule of Sicily.
