1 CONT GIVN Blanca Alphonsa
1 CONT NSFX Queen of France
1 CONT AFN 8XJ4-TP
1 CONT DATE 25 APR 2000
1 CONT TIME 20:45:48
1 CONT It is reported that Blanche could be quite domineering and as such pro tested when her son Louis IX went to the Crusades.  Blanche of Castil e (March 4, 1188 - November 26, 1252), wife of Louis VIII of France. S he was born at Pal
1 CONC encia, the third daughter of Alfonso VIII, king of C astile, and of Eleanor of England. Eleanor was a daughter of Henry II of England and his Queen consort Eleanor of Aquitaine.  Despite her An gevin ancestry, Blanche was complete
1 CONC ly French.
1 CONT
1 CONT In consequence of a treaty between Philip Augustus and John of England , she was betrothed to the former's son, Louis, and was brought to Fra nce, in the spring of 1200, by John's mother Eleanor. On May 22, 1200 the treaty was fin
1 CONC ally signed, John ceding with his niece the fiefs o f Issoudun and Gracay, together with those that André de Chauvigny, lo rd of Châteauroux, held in Berry, of the English crown. The marriage w as celebrated the next day, at Portm
1 CONC ort on the right bank of the Seine , in John's domains, as those of Philip lay under an interdict.
1 CONT
1 CONT Blanche first displayed her great qualities in 1216, when Louis, who o n the death of John claimed the English crown in her right, invaded En gland, only to find a united nation against him.  It is reported that Blanche was in com
1 CONC plete support of her husband (Louis VIII) in his inv asion of England.  She took charge of attempts to send supplies acros s the channel.  Philip Augustus refused to help his son, and Blanche w as his sole support. The queen estab
1 CONC lished herself at Calais and organ ized two fleets, one of which was commanded by Eustace the Monk, and a n army under Robert of Courtenay; but all her resolution and energy we re in vain. Although it would seem that her masterful
1 CONC  temper exercise d a sensible influence upon her husband's gentler character, her role during his brief reign (1223-1226) is not well known.
1 CONT
1 CONT Upon Louis's death he left Blanche regent and guardian of his children .  As regent she took over administration of the kingdom with a stron g hand.  During her regency, Raimond VII of Toulouse, son of Raimond V I was defeated and
1 CONC  the Albigensian hersey was wiped out.  Blanche als o arrange that one of her younger sons would marry Raimond VI's daught er - his heir.  But soon opposition began to arise against the Queen m other.  As Queen, she played a more 
1 CONC significant role in the governmen t of the realm than her predecessors.  Of her twelve or thirteen child ren, six had died, and Louis, the heir- afterwards the sainted Louis I X - was but twelve years old. The situation was critic
1 CONC al, for the hard -won domains of the house of Capet seemed likely to fall to pieces dur ing a minority. Blanche had to bear the whole burden of affairs alone , to break up a league of the barons (1226), and to repel the attack o f
1 CONC  Henry III King of England at St. Malo in Bretagne on 3 May, 1230, th en in support of French barons opposed to Blanche.  In the face of th e attack by Henry III, Blanche summoned the vassels of Louis IX (for w hom she was regent)
1 CONC  to give assistance against Henry III.  But her ene rgy and firmness overcame all dangers. There was an end to the calumni es circulated against her, based on the poetical homage rendered her b y Theobald IV of Champagne, and the 
1 CONC prolonged stay in Paris of thepapa l legate, Romano Bonaventura, cardinal of Sant' Angelo.
1 CONT
1 CONT From 1226, until her death in 1252, she held great sway within the rea lm.  After her husbands death, Blanche retained the services of her hu sbands Hospitaller Guerin and Renaud de Roye.  Between 1226 and 1234, she acted as regen
1 CONC t while Louis IX was a minor and again from 1248 unt il 1252 she acted as regent again during Louis's absence on the crusad e.  During these years, Blanche is styled as having a 'severe personal ity' and was 'heroic in nature'.  S
1 CONC he was able to break up hte coaliti on of barons led by Peter Maruclerc - Count of Brittany and Raymond VI I - Count of Toulouse and was able to fend off Henry III of England wh o tried to reclaim his ancesteral lands lost by John
1 CONC  Lackland.  Durin g this period she was also able to establish French royal power in sou thern France.  She was also responsible for the suppression of a revol t in the Languedoc region of France.
1 CONT
1 CONT The nobles were awed by her warlike preparations or won over by adroi t diplomacy, and their league was broken up. St Louis owed his realm t o his mother, but he himself always remained somewhat under the spello f her imperious pe
1 CONC rsonality. After he came of age (1236) her influenc e upon him may still be traced. In 1248 she again became regent, durin g Louis IX's absence on the crusade, a project which she had stronglyo pposed. In the disasters which follo
1 CONC wed she maintained peace, while dr aining the land of men and money to aid her son in the East. At lasthe r strength failed her. She fell ill into a bail of hay at Melun in Nov ember 1252, and was taken to Paris, but lived only a 
1 CONC few days. She wa s buried at Maubuisson.
1 CONT
1 CONT Besides the works of Joinville and William of Nangis, see Elie Berger , "Histoire de Blanche de Castille, reine de France," in Bibliothèqued es écoles francaises d’Athènes et de Rome, vol. lxx. (Paris, 1895); L e Nain de Tillemont
1 CONC , "Vie de Saint Louis," ed. by J de Gaulle for theS ociété de l’histoire de France (6 vols., 1847-1851); and Paulin Paris , "Nouvelles recherches sur les mteurs de la reine Blanche et de Thiba ud," in Cabinet historigue (1858).
