0 @N6000000002248195612@ NOTE RICHARD I COEUR DE LION ('THE LIONHEART') (r. 1189-1199)
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's elder son, Richard I (reigned 1189-99), fulfilled his main ambition by going on crusade in 1190, leaving the ruling of England to others. After his victories over Saladin at the siege of Acre and the battles of Arsuf and J
1 CONC affa, concluded by the treaty of Jaffa (1192), Richard was returning from the Holy Land when he was captured in Austria. In early 1193, Richard was transferred to Emperor Henry VI's custody.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Richard's absence, King Philip of France failed to obtain Richard's French possessions through invasion or negotiation. In England, Richard's brother John occupied Windsor Castle and prepared an invasion of England by Flemish m
1 CONC ercenaries, accompanied by armed uprisings. Their mother, Queen Eleanor, took firm action against John by strengthening garrisons and again exacting oaths of allegiance to the king. John's subversive activities were ended by the p
1 CONC ayment of a crushing ransom of 150,000 marks of silver to the emperor, for Richard's release in 1194. Warned by Philip's famous message 'look to yourself, the devil is loosed', John fled to the French court. 
1 CONT On his return to England, Richard was recrowned at Winchester in 1194. Five years later he died in France during a minor siege against a rebellious baron. By the time of his death, Richard had recovered all his lands. His success 
1 CONC was short-lived. In 1199 his brother John became king and Philip successfully invaded Normandy. By 1203, John had retreated to England, losing his French lands of Normandy and Anjou by 1205
1 CONT
1 CONT Source: http://www.royal.gov.uk/output/Page63.asp
0 @N6000000001744974690@ NOTE Ruled 1189-1199. As Henry's eldest son, RIchard became ing in 1189 and promptly set out for the Holy Land to join the Third Crusade. Failing to conquer Jerusalem, he was captured on the way home and held to ransom for more than a 
1 CONC year by the emperor Henry VI. While Richard was in prison, his brother John has ceded territory to Philip of France. On his release, Richard entrusted the governemtn of England to the Archbishop of Canterbury rather than his broth
1 CONC er, and devoted the next five years to trying to recover his French possession. he was still doing this when he died of wounds in 1199. An outstanding soldier, Richard spent less than a year in England during his reign.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Nicholas Best, "The Kings and Queens of England" (Boston: Bullfinch, 1995).
1 CONT
1 CONT Sharon Penman, in her novel "Here Be Dragons" (1985), states that Richard was homosexual and that the marriage to Berengaria was arranged by his mother Eleanor of Aquitaine in hopes of procuring an heir, but was without issue.
1 CONT
1 CONT One source named two sons, Phillip of Cognac and Fulk.
0 @N6000000003122792883@ NOTE While Richard Plantagenet is revered as one of the great warrior kings of England, he is perhaps best known as "the absent king." This is due to the fact that during his reign from 1189-1199, he spent a total of six months in Engl
1 CONC and. This aside Richard I was well known for his bravery which earned him the nickname "The Lionheart". A name that has reached epic and mythological proportions, best seen in literary works such as Robin Hood and Sir Walter Scott
1 CONC 's novel Ivanhoe.
1 CONT Richard Plantagenet came into the world September 8th in the year 1157 AD Although born in Oxfordshire England, Richard was a child of Aquitaine a part of Southern France. His native language was not English and throughout his lif
1 CONC e he spoke little of it.
1 CONT He had four brothers and three sisters, the first of which died at a young age. Of the remainder; Henry was named heir to the English throne, Richard was to succeed his mother's Aquitaine and Geoffrey was to inherit Brittany. John
1 CONC  was the poorest to fair out receiving nothing from his father. It is this action that gave him the name John Lackland.
1 CONT At a young age of twelve, Richard pledged homage to the king of France for lands of his. At the age of fourteen, Richard was named the Duke of Aquitaine in the church of St. Hillaire at Poitiers which was one of the lands made hom
1 CONC age to the French King. Henry's sons, who had been given lands but no real power revolted against their King father aided by their mother. In retaliation King Henry had Eleanor jailed. She remained there for many years.
1 CONT In 1183 the younger Henry died leaving Richard as the heir to the English throne. Another family dispute occurred when Richard received the lands of his brother. Henry was expected to give his Aquitaine to his brother John. Richar
1 CONC d refused to give up the homeland of his mother. While this dispute over family land raged on, Richard learned of the tragic loss at Hattin, where the Crusaders had lost Jerusalem to the Saracen leader Saladin. Richard soon took u
1 CONC p the cross of the crusades, much against his father's approval.
1 CONT In 1189, upon the death of Henry II, Richard was crowned king of England in Westminster Abbey London. One of his first actions was to free his mother from prison. His second was to begin to raise funds for his crusade known to his
1 CONC tory as the Third Crusade. He imposed a tax on the English people called a Saladin tithe as a means of aiding his war effort. 
1 CONT After the Third Crusade, Richard began his homeward journey to England. Put ashore by bad weather he found himself in Austria home of Leopold, whom Richard had angered by actions during the crusade. Leopold captured King Richard a
1 CONC nd imprisoned him in his castle. Eager for a piece of the action the Emperor of Germany offered Leopold 75,000 marks for Richard taking him into custody in Germany.
1 CONT Rumors ran rampant throughout England over the missing king. There is a legend that the troubadour Blondel heard his king singing in a castle and responded with a song that the both of them were sure to know. Whether true or not t
1 CONC he fact remains that two Abbots were soon dispatched to journey for him through the network of the church. Even Eleanor, Richard's mother wrote to the Pope for assistance in the matter. Richard was found and soon a ransom was set 
1 CONC for his return to England. The sum was 150,000 marks an amount equal to three years of annual income and weighing at three tons in silver.
1 CONT Richard returned to England receiving a hero's welcome. He forgave his brother John, by saying he was manipulated by cunning people and vowed to punish them and not his brother. Unfortunately for the King he returned to a land in 
1 CONC financial troubles. The cost of the Crusade and his large ransom had tapped out the finances of the land. This monetary trouble was to plague him for his remaining five-year reign. He created a new great seal as a means to raise f
1 CONC unds and made void all documents signed with the old.
1 CONT For such a brave and noble man, King Richard's death came about in a rather strange way. In Chalus, Aquitaine, a peasant plowing his fields came upon a treasure. This treasure consisted of some gold statues and coins. The feudal l
1 CONC ord claimed the treasure from his vassal, Richard in turn claimed the treasure from the lord, who refused. This prompted Richard to siege the village.
1 CONT During the siege Richard was riding close to the castle without the protection of full armor. He spotted an archer with bow in hand on the wall aiming a shot at him. It is said Richard paused to applaud the Bowman. He was struck i
1 CONC n the shoulder with the arrow and refused treatment for his wound. Infection set in and Richard the Lionheart died on April the 6th 1199. He was buried in the Fontvraud Abbey in Anjou France.
0 @N6000000003177905276@ NOTE 1  AUTH Sl
0 @N6000000005076664311@ NOTE Richard I 'Coeur de Lion', King of England was born on 8 September 1157 at Beaumont Palace, Oxford, Oxfordshire, England.1 He was the son of Henry II 'Curtmantle' d'Anjou, King of England and Eleanor, Duchesse d'Aquitaine. He marr
1 CONC ied Berengaria de Navarre, daughter of Sancho VI, Rey de Navarre and Sanchia de Castilla, on 12 May 1191 at Chapel of St. George, Limassol, Cyprus.1 He died on 6 April 1199 at age 41 at Chalus, Limousin, France.3 He was buried at 
1 CONC Fontevraud Abbey, Fontevraud, France.3
1 CONT      Richard I 'Coeur de Lion', King of England gained the title of Duc d'Aquitaine in 1172.1 He succeeded to the title of King Richard I of England on 6 July 1189.1 He was crowned King of England on 3 September 1189 at Westminste
1 CONC r Abbey, Westminster, London, England, and styled 'Rex Anglaie, Dux Normanniae et Aquitainaie et Comes Andegavaie.4'
1 CONT      He spent his youth in France training as a soldier and later fought his father, Henry II, in England. In fact, he spent only six months of his reign in England. He married Berengaria of Naverre when on a Crusade in 1191. Duri
1 CONC ng this Crusade he captured Cyprus. On his way home he was taken prisoner in Austria and held to ransom. There is a nice legend that his faithful minstrel Blondel went from castle to castle strumming the notes of his master's favo
1 CONC urite melody. Upon reaching the place of the King's imprisonment he was delighted to hear the answering response from his master's harp. Richard spent only two short periods of his reign in England, the rest of time he was away on
1 CONC  Crusades or in France. He was impetuous, brave and forgiving but had no political aptitude. He has an extensive biographical entry in the Dictionary of National Biography.5
1 CONT
1 CONT Child of Richard I 'Coeur de Lion', King of England and Joan de St. Pol
1 CONT Fulk (?) 6
