0 @N6000000000337394791@ NOTE Source #1:  Frederick Lewis Weis, "Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists Who Came to America Before 1700" - Seventh Edition, with additions and corrections by Walter Lee Sheppard, Jr., assisted by Davis Faris (Baltimore, M
1 CONC D:  Genealogical Publishing Co, 1995), p. 102
1 CONT
1 CONT King of Castile (1217-1252) and of Leon (1230-1252);  canonized by Pope Clement X in 1671 
0 @N6000000000436936472@ NOTE King of Castile and Leon
0 @N6000000001563260093@ NOTE Conquered Cordoba and Seville from the Moors
0 @N6000000001578904364@ NOTE Conquered Cordoba and Seville from the Moors
0 @N6000000002319313022@ NOTE Acceded: 1217
0 @N6000000002322264753@ NOTE Acceded: 1217
1 CONT
1 CONT St. Ferdinand III of Castile
1 CONT Feastday: May 30 Patron of engineers
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand III of Castile was the son of Alfonso IX, King of Leon, and Berengaria, daughter of Alfonso III, King of Castile (Spain). He was declared king of Castile at age eighteen. Ferdinand was born near Salamanca; proclaimed kin
1 CONC g of Palencia, Valladolid, and Burgos; his mother advised and assisted him during his young reign. He married Princess Beatrice, daughter of Philip of Suabia, King of Germany and they had seven sons and three daughters. His father
1 CONC  (the king of Leon) turned against him and tried to take over his rule. The two reconciled later, and fought successfully against the Moors. In 1225, he held back Islamic invaders; prayed and fasted to prepare for the war; extreme
1 CONC ly devoted to the Blessed Virgin. Between 1234-36, Ferdinand conquered the city of Cordoba from the Moors. Queen Beatrice died in 1236, and he overtook Seville shortly thereafter. He founded the Cathedral of Burgos and the Univers
1 CONC ity of Salamanca; married Joan of Ponthieu after the death of Beatrice. He died on May 30th after a prolonged illness, and buried in the habit of his secular Franciscan Order. His remains are preserved in the Cathedral of Seville 
1 CONC and was canonized by Pope Clement X in 1671. Ferdinand was a great administrator and a man of deep faith. He founded hospitals and bishoprics, monasteries, chuches, and cathedrals during his reign. Her also compiled and reformed a
1 CONC  code of laws which were used until the modern era. Ferdinand rebuilt the Cathedral of Burgos and changed the mosque in Seville into a Cathedral. He was a just ruler, frequently pardoning former offenders to his throne. His feast 
1 CONC day is May 30th.
0 @N6000000002187750916@ NOTE Ferdinand III of Castile
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fernando III called El Santo (the Saint), (1198/1199 – May 30, 1252) was a king of Castile (1217 - 1252) and Leon (1230 - 1252). He was the son of Alfonso IX and Berenguela of Castile, daughter of Alfonso VIII. Because his parents
1 CONC  were first cousins, their marriage was annulled, but Fernando remained legitimized and was able to succeed his father as king.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1231 he united Castile and Leon permanently.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fernando spent much of his reign fighting the Moors. He captured the towns of Córdoba in 1236, Jaén in 1246, and Seville in 1248, and occupied Murcia in 1243, thereby completing the reconquest of Spain excepting Granada, whose kin
1 CONC g nevertheless did homage to Fernando.
1 CONT
1 CONT He founded the University of Salamanca and the Cathedral of Burgos.
1 CONT
1 CONT Fernando was canonized by Pope Clement X in 1671. Several places named San Fernando were founded across the Spanish Empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Marriages and Family
1 CONT In 1219, Ferdinand married the daughter of the German king Philip of Swabia, Elizabeth, called Beatriz in Spain. Their children were:
1 CONT
1 CONT King Alfonso X of Castile (November 23, 1221-1284) 
1 CONT Infante Fadrique (September 1223-1277), secretly executed by his brother Alfonso. 
1 CONT Infante Fernando (March 1225-1243/1248) 
1 CONT Infanta Leonor (1227-died young) 
1 CONT Infanta Berenguela, a nun at las Huelgas (1228-1288/89). 
1 CONT Infante Enrique "El Senador" (March 1230-August 1304) 
1 CONT Infante Felipe (December 1231-1274). He was promised to the Church, but was so taken with the beauty of Princess Christine of Norway (daughter of Haakon IV of Norway), who had been intended as a bride for one of his brothers, that
1 CONC  he abandoned his holy vows and married her. She died in 1262, childless. 
1 CONT Infante Sancho, Archbishop of Toledo and Seville (1233-1261) 
1 CONT Infante Juan Manuel (1234-November 1283). 
1 CONT Infanta Maria, died an infant in November 1235. 
1 CONT After Elizabeth died in 1235, he married Jeanne de Dammartin, Countess of Ponthieu, before August 1237. They had four sons and one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT Infante Fernando, Count of Aumale (1239-1269) 
1 CONT Eleanor of Castile (1241-1290), wife of King Edward I of England. 
1 CONT Infante Luis (1243-1269) 
1 CONT Infante Ximen (1244), died young and buried in a monastery in Toledo. 
1 CONT Infante Juan (1245), died young and buried at the cathedral in Cordoba. 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT Gonzalez, Julio. Reinado y Diplomas de Fernando III, i: Estudio, 1980 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry I King of Castile
1 CONT 1217-1252 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Alfonso X 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Alfonso IX King of Leon
1 CONT 1230-1252 
0 @N6000000002134159553@ NOTE Ferdinand III, 1199?1252, Spanish king of Castile (1217?52) and León (1230?52), son of Alfonso IX of León and Berenguela of Castile. At the death (1217) of her brother, Henry I of Castile, Berenguela renounced her right of success
1 CONC ion in Ferdinand's favor. Having inherited (1230) León from his father, Ferdinand permanently united the kingdoms of Castile and León. Ferdinand spent most of his reign crusading against the Moors. He took Córdoba (1236), Jaén (12
1 CONC 46), and Seville (1248) and occupied Murcia (1243). He thus completed the reconquest of Spain, except for the kingdom of Granada, which became a vassal state. Ferdinand was planning an expedition to Morocco when he died and was su
1 CONC cceeded by his son, Alfonso X. In 1671, Ferdinand was canonized by the Roman Catholic Church. Feast: May 30.
0 @N6000000002176622469@ NOTE Acceded: 1217
0 @N6000000002176683031@ NOTE The son of Alfonso IX of Leon, his mother, Berengaria of Castile, renounced
1 CONT her castilian throne in favor of her son. This enraged her husband who
1 CONT declared war on his newly crowned son. But, Ferdinand proved more than a
1 CONT military match for Alfonso.
0 @N6000000002322339452@ NOTE The son of Alfonso IX of Leon, his mother, Berengaria of Castile, renounced
1 CONT her castilian throne in favor of her son. This enraged her husband who
1 CONT declared war on his newly crowned son. But, Ferdinand proved more than a
1 CONT military match for Alfonso.
0 @N6000000001956401353@ NOTE
1 CONT --canonized February 4, 1671; feast day May 30 
1 CONT --also called Saint Ferdinand, Spanish San Fernandoking of Castile from 1217 to 1252 and of Leon from 1230 to 1252 and conqueror of the Muslim cities of Córdoba (1236), Jaén (1246), and Sevilla (1248). During his campaigns, Murcia
1 CONC  submitted to his son Alfonso (later Alfonso X), and the Muslim kingdom of Granada became his vassal.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand was the son of Alfonso IX of Leon and Berenguela, daughter of Alfonso VIII ofCastile. At birth, he was the heir to Leon, but his uncle, Henry I of Castile, died young, and his mother inherited the crown of Castile, which
1 CONC  she conferred on him. His father, like many Leonese, opposed the union, and Ferdinand found himself at war with him. By his will Alfonso IX tried to disinherit his son, but the will was set aside, and Castile and Leon were perman
1 CONC ently united in 1230.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand married Beatrice of Swabia, daughter of the Holy Roman emperor, a title that Ferdinand's son Alfonso X was to claim. His conquest of Lower Andalusia was the resultof the disintegration of the Almohad state. The Castilian
1 CONC s and other conquerors occupied the cities, driving out the Muslims and taking over vast estates.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ferdinand's second wife was Joan of Ponthieu, whom he married in 1237; their daughter Eleanor married the future Edward I of England in 1254. Ferdinand settled in Sevilla, where he is buried.
